window.CARDS.push({
id:'27', n:27, w:1,
q:'Explain the process of photosynthesis.',
qcn:'解释光合作用的过程',
sub:'主干说完约 90 秒 · §9.1（p202–p212）· 卡 25 的镜像：同一台合酶、同一个梯度，只是光把电子推上坡',

/* ---------------- 速背 ---------------- */
cram:[
 {g:'开口', gn:'题面只有两个实词。<b>photosynthesis</b> 要先给定义，再拆成两半；<b>process</b> 是他要的形状——按<b>顺序</b>走完一条路，不是报一串名词。他嘴里多半只有 “What is photosynthesis?”，剩下的结构要你自己接：是什么 → 总式 → 两半 → 在哪儿跑 → 然后才是载体的名字。<b>这一章她没学过，所以前三点是学，不是提醒。</b>'},
 {n:'01', t:'定义 · 什么是光合作用',
  big:'把<b>光能变成化学能</b>的生物学转换：光同时驱动<b>水的氧化</b>和 <b>CO₂ 的还原</b>，产物是糖和 O₂。<b>氧来自水，不是 CO₂</b>',
  en:'“<b>Photosynthesis is the biological conversion of radiant energy into chemical energy</b> — that is this textbook’s own opening sentence. Light drives two things at once: the <b>oxidation of a donor</b> and the <b>reduction of carbon dioxide</b> to carbohydrate. The book writes the most general form as <b>H₂D plus A, over the pigments, giving H₂A plus D</b>, where H₂D is the donor of electrons and protons and A is the acceptor — and it says that for every photosynthesising organism the acceptor A is carbon dioxide. In green plants the donor is <b>water</b>, which is why oxygen is released; some bacteria use hydrogen sulfide instead and deposit <b>sulfur</b>.”',
  note:'<b>先说它是什么，别一上来就 “light reactions and dark reactions”</b>——那是分类，不是定义。书 p202 §9.1 原话：<i>fotosyntézu, chápanou jako biologickou přeměnu zářivé energie v energii chemickou</i>，通式 H₂D + A → H₂A + D（已回 400 dpi 核过）。细菌那条式子书也印了：H₂S + CO₂ → (CH₂O) + S。<br><b>「通式」这一句是全卡最值钱的开口</b>：它一句话把植物、蓝细菌和硫细菌装进同一个框，还顺带解释了氧为什么来自水——<b>水在式子里站的是 H₂D 的位置，被氧化的那一边</b>。<span class="pg">p202</span>'},
 {n:'02', t:'书的总式 ＋ 氧来自水',
  big:'每个碳：<b>2 H₂O ＋ CO₂ → (CH₂O) ＋ O₂ ＋ H₂O</b>；一分子己糖：<b>12 H₂O ＋ 6 CO₂ → C₆H₁₂O₆ ＋ 6 O₂ ＋ 6 H₂O</b>，<b>ΔG°′ = +2867 kJ/mol</b>',
  en:'“Per carbon the textbook writes <b>two waters plus CO₂ give one carbohydrate unit, one oxygen and one water</b>; for a hexose, <b>twelve waters and six CO₂ give glucose, six oxygens and six waters</b>, with a standard Gibbs energy of <b>plus 2867 kilojoules per mole</b>. It writes twelve waters rather than six on purpose — its own words are that the stoichiometric factors are given <b>so as to stress that the six oxygen molecules released come from water and not from carbon dioxide</b>. To make one O₂ you must photolyse two waters.”',
  warn:'<b>正号。</b>+2867 是<b>吸能</b>的——这就是为什么非要有光。别把它说成 −2867，那等于把光合说成了呼吸。',
  good:'顺势接一句镜像：“And the same book gives the complete oxidation of glucose as <b>minus 2870</b> kilojoules per mole — the two numbers are the same energy read in opposite directions.”（−2870 在 <span class="pg">p135</span> 的表里）',
  note:'两条式子和 ΔG°′ <b>已回 400 dpi 逐字核过</b>（OCR 把 2 867 读成了别的）。<b>「氧来自水」是这道题最常考的一句</b>，而书把它写成了写式子的<b>理由</b>，不是脚注——引用书的这个动机比引用结论有力。<span class="pg">p203</span>'},
 {n:'03', t:'两半 ＋ 在哪儿跑（含「暗」这个陷阱）',
  big:'<b>光反应</b>在<b>类囊体膜</b>上，产 <b>ATP ＋ NADPH ＋ O₂</b>；<b>固定 CO₂</b>在<b>基质</b>里，把这两样花掉。「dark」= <b>不直接需要光</b>，<b>不是「夜里」</b>——书自己说这个名字不合适',
  en:'“The process has two halves in two places. The <b>light reactions</b> run in the <b>thylakoid membrane</b> — thylakos is Greek for a sac, and the thylakoids are closed flattened vesicles stacked inside the chloroplast — and their first stable products are <b>ATP and NADPH</b>, with oxygen released as a by-product of splitting water. The <b>fixation and reduction of CO₂</b> runs in the <b>stroma</b>, the soluble phase outside the thylakoids, and spends that ATP and NADPH. This textbook actively objects to the name <b>dark reactions</b>: its own words are that the term is <b>not appropriate, because it suggests the conversion does not occur in the light</b>. CO₂ fixation runs in light or in darkness for as long as there is enough ATP and NADPH — so ‘dark’ means <b>not directly requiring light</b>, not ‘at night’.”',
  note:'<b>叶绿体的结构，Obr. 9.1 的标注（已回 400 dpi 核过）</b>：外膜 · 内膜 · 膜间隙 · <b>granum</b>（一摞类囊体）· <b>stromální thylakoidy</b>（连接两摞的基质类囊体）· <b>stroma</b> · <b>lumen＝类囊体的内腔</b>。<br>⚠ <b>全书正文 grep「granum」0 命中，这个词只在图上。</b>所以它是<b>图承载的锚点</b>——说的时候可以加一句 “the textbook gives the grana in its diagram of a maize chloroplast”。<br><b>他可能让你画叶绿体。五步，边画边说</b>：① 画一个椭圆，外面<b>两条</b>贴着的线 = 外膜和内膜，中间那条缝写 <b>intermembrane space</b>；② 椭圆内部空白处写 <b>stroma</b>（这里放 rubisco 和整个 Calvin 循环）；③ 在里面画两摞压扁的饼，每摞标 <b>granum</b>，一摞里的每一片就是一个 <b>thylakoid</b>；④ 用长条把两摞连起来，标 <b>stroma thylakoid</b>；⑤ 在一片类囊体里面点一下，写 <b>lumen</b>，箭头<b>向内</b>写 <b>H⁺</b>。<b>画完这五步，「光反应在哪、暗反应在哪、质子往哪去」三个问题一起答完了。</b><span class="pg">p202 p203 p209</span>'},

 {g:'账', gn:'投入 / 产出 / 总结三行，缺一不可。只报产出是半张账——他一句「那消耗了什么」就能把你问停。<b>这张账要分两侧记：光反应造账，固定 CO₂ 花账。</b>'},
 {n:'04', t:'投入',
  big:'<b>光反应</b>：2 <b>H₂O</b> ＋ <b>光子</b> ＋ ADP ＋ Pᵢ ＋ NADP⁺ · <b>Calvin（每个 CO₂）</b>：1 <b>CO₂</b> ＋ 1 <b>RuBP</b>（被回收）＋ <b>3 ATP</b> ＋ <b>2 NADPH</b>',
  en:'“In go, on the light side, <b>water, photons, ADP with inorganic phosphate, and NADP-plus</b>; the textbook’s figure writes the water step as <b>two H₂O giving four protons and one O₂</b>. On the Calvin side, per carbon dioxide fixed: <b>one CO₂, one ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, three ATP and two NADPH</b>. I should say that the three and the two are the standard figures — <b>this textbook nowhere prints an ATP and NADPH count</b>; it only says the reduction step spends ATP and NADPH and that the regeneration step spends ATP.”',
  warn:'<b>3 ATP ＋ 2 NADPH 是通用数，书里没有。</b>报的时候带一句 “those are the standard numbers, not this book’s”。书<b>确实印了</b>的数只有图上那几个：2 H₂O → 4 H⁺ ＋ O₂、<b>3 个质子换 1 个 ATP</b>、2 NADP⁺ ＋ 2 H⁺ → 2 NADPH。',
  note:'<b>RuBP 不算净投入</b>——它在第 1 步被消耗、在再生阶段被造回来，跟柠檬酸循环里的 oxaloacetate 一样是<b>被回收的</b>，不是原料。这一点他很可能追，跟卡 <b>24</b> 是同一招。<span class="pg">p207 p209 p210</span>'},
 {n:'05', t:'产出',
  big:'<b>光反应</b>：<b>ATP ＋ NADPH</b>（书叫它们「第一批稳定产物」）＋ <b>O₂</b> · <b>Calvin（每个 CO₂）</b>：2 × <b>3-phosphoglycerate</b> → 2 GAP，净出<b>一个碳</b>；ADP ＋ Pᵢ ＋ NADP⁺ 还回去',
  en:'“Out come, on the light side, <b>ATP and NADPH</b> — the textbook’s phrase is that the thylakoid processes give <b>NADPH and ATP as the first stable products, in which the electromagnetic energy of the photons is stored as chemical energy</b> — plus <b>oxygen</b>. On the Calvin side, each CO₂ gives <b>two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate</b>, a three-carbon acid, which are reduced to two glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate; the ADP, phosphate and NADP-plus go back to the membrane. The textbook states the <b>net yield of the Calvin cycle as one molecule of fructose-6-phosphate</b>, which is then turned into glucose-6-phosphate and on into glucose, cellulose or glycolysis.”',
  note:'<b>「第一批稳定产物」这个说法很好用</b>：它把光反应的产出说成<b>能量的储存形式</b>，而不是「一堆分子」。<span class="pg">p209 p211 p212</span>'},
 {n:'06', t:'总结（净账）',
  big:'<b>光反应造账，暗反应花账。</b>每固定 1 个 CO₂ 花 <b>3 ATP ＋ 2 NADPH</b>（通用数）；<b>六圈</b>才净出一个己糖：<b>6 CO₂ ＋ 12 H₂O ＋ 18 ATP ＋ 12 NADPH → 1 己糖 ＋ 6 O₂</b>。<b>碳全部来自 CO₂，氧全部来自水，还原力也来自水</b>',
  en:'“The net of it: the light reactions make <b>ATP and NADPH and nothing else that lasts</b>; the Calvin cycle consumes them and fixes carbon. Per CO₂ that is three ATP and two NADPH, so <b>six turns</b> — six CO₂ — are needed for one hexose, eighteen ATP and twelve NADPH. Those counts are the standard ones and not in this textbook; what <b>is</b> in it is the overall equation, twelve waters and six CO₂ giving a hexose, six oxygens and six waters, at plus 2867 kilojoules per mole. Said in one line: <b>every carbon in the sugar comes from CO₂, every oxygen molecule released comes from water, and so does the reducing power.</b>”',
  warn:'书自己警告了总式的<b>两个误读</b>（p209，值钱）：① 六个 CO₂ 的碳<b>不是</b>都进同一个糖分子；② 固定 CO₂ 的去向<b>不只是</b>造己糖——<b>更大的一部分被送进中央代谢通路（书点名了柠檬酸循环）</b>，给氨基酸、蛋白质、脂肪酸、核苷酸、色素当碳架和能源。英文：“The textbook warns that the summary equation invites two wrong readings: that the six carbons end up in one and the same sugar, and that hexose is the point of it. Most of the fixed carbon goes into the central pathways — it names the citric acid cycle — as carbon skeletons for amino acids, fatty acids, nucleotides and pigments.”',
  note:'<b>这一格是整道题最容易被问、也最容易只答一半的地方。</b>「六圈」那句能挡住 “how many turns for one glucose”。<span class="pg">p203 p209</span>'},

 {g:'色素', gn:'三类，书自己编了号。<b>每一类只记三件事：结构的核心、在光合里干什么、书给的例子。</b>结构式不用背（她另有 app），但<b>卟啉环 ＋ 中心的镁 ＋ 植醇尾</b>这三个词必须能说出口。'},
 {n:'07', t:'叶绿素的结构',
  big:'一个<b>卟啉</b>（四个吡咯环用<b>甲川桥</b>连成的大环，共轭双键很多），中心是 <b>Mg²⁺</b>，外加一条 <b>C₂₀ 的植醇（phytol）尾巴</b>。<b>卟啉负责光化学，尾巴负责插在膜里</b>',
  en:'“The textbook puts chlorophylls in the first of its three classes of photosynthetic pigment and says they are <b>porphyrins — molecules similar to heme, as in haemoglobin and the cytochromes</b>. A porphyrin is <b>four pyrrole rings joined to each other by methine bridges, with a rich system of conjugated double bonds</b>; that conjugated system is what absorbs visible light. At the centre of chlorophyll sits a <b>magnesium</b> ion where heme has iron. Besides the porphyrin, which the book says is <b>always the part responsible for the photochemical properties</b>, the molecule carries an alcohol: most often the <b>phytyl residue of twenty carbons</b>, an isoprenoid — a long hydrophobic tail that anchors the pigment in the membrane.”',
  good:'他要证据：“The book’s own evidence for the magnesium is in the legend of figure 9.5, where it defines <b>pheophytin as chlorophyll without the Mg ion</b> — and pheophytin is a real carrier in photosystem II.”',
  note:'<b>正文没写 Mg，图上写了。</b>Obr. 9.2（p204，已回 400 dpi 核过）画的中心就是 <b>Mg</b>，四个环标着 I–IV，右下引出 <b>fytol</b>；Obr. 9.5 图注写 <i>Pheo — feofytin (chlorofyl bez iontu Mg)</i>。<br><b>接回两张已有的卡</b>：卟啉和血红素在横向卡<b>「化学键」</b>里（铁被四个氮的<b>配位键</b>抱住，p037）；<b>phytol 是二萜醇</b>在卡 <b>23</b>（脂）里（p183 明说「醇 fytol 是高等植物叶绿素 a 和 b 的组成部分」）；<b>血红素的四吡咯 ＋ 甲川桥</b>在卡 <b>25</b> 的细胞色素那一格（p046）。<b>说 “same ring as heme, different metal” 一句话就把三张卡连起来了。</b><span class="pg">p046 p183 p204 p205</span>'},
 {n:'08', t:'chlorophyll a 与 b · 为什么植物是绿的',
  big:'<b>a 是唯一真正做能量转换的</b>，b 只是把能量递给它。差别在<b>第 II 环上一个基团</b>：a 是 <b>–CH₃</b>，b 是 <b>–CHO</b>。绿色是<b>剩下来的</b>：叶绿素吃掉<b>蓝（415–440 nm）</b>和<b>红（640–700 nm）</b>，中间那段<b>没被吸走的</b>就是绿',
  en:'“<b>Chlorophyll a</b> is universal in organisms with oxygenic photosynthesis, and the textbook says this led to the correct view that <b>only chlorophyll a is the actual transformer of energy</b> — the one that takes part directly in the primary photochemical act. <b>Chlorophyll b is an accessory pigment</b>: it absorbs and passes the energy on to chlorophyll a. Structurally they differ at <b>one substituent on ring two — a methyl group in a, a formyl group in b</b>. As for the colour: chlorophyll absorbs in the blue, <b>415 to 440 nanometres</b>, and in the red, <b>640 to 700</b>. The textbook’s sentence is that <b>the complementary colour of red is green, and that is why the plant world is green</b> — the green light in between is the part that is <b>not</b> absorbed, so it is reflected and reaches your eye.”',
  warn:'<b>书有一处自己跟自己拉扯，值得知道。</b>p205 说植物是绿的因为绿是红的补色；但 Obr. 9.4 的图注说这些色素<b>「实际上覆盖了可见区的整个太阳光谱」</b>。两句话怎么同时成立：图里填补 500–600 nm 那一段的是 <b>fykoerythrin 和 fykocyanin</b>，而书自己说<b>那是蓝细菌和红藻的色素</b>。<b>高等植物只有叶绿素 a、b 和类胡萝卜素，绿的那一段照样是空的。</b>英文：“The figure says the pigments cover almost the whole visible spectrum, but the ones filling the green gap are the phycobilins of cyanobacteria and red algae — a higher plant has only the chlorophylls and the carotenoids, so the gap stays open and the leaf looks green.”',
  note:'<b>书还说了一件很多人不知道的事</b>：415–440 nm 那个蓝带<b>「对光合不重要」</b>，因为它把电子激发到<b>第二单重激发态</b>，寿命太短，来不及启动后面的氧化还原反应；有用的是红带，对应<b>第一单重激发态</b>。<br>⚠ <b>通用说法跟书不同</b>：现代说法是分子在飞秒内从 S₂ 内转换降到 S₁，所以蓝光<b>照样能用</b>，只是每个蓝光子最后只值一个红光子的能量，多出来的变成热。<b>先给书的，被追问再给这一层，并说明是 general。</b><br>a 与 b 的取代基差别<b>取自 Obr. 9.2 圈出来的那一块</b>（已回 400 dpi 核过），正文没有写。<span class="pg">p204 p205 p206</span>'},
 {n:'09', t:'类胡萝卜素：辅助 ＋ 保护',
  big:'第三类色素，<b>四萜（isoprenoid）</b>，靠 <b>3–11 个共轭双键</b>显色。两个作用：<b>①</b> 补上叶绿素吸不到的那一段光，把能量递进反应中心；<b>②</b> <b>结合活性氧</b>，当抗氧化剂——这就是「保护色素」的出处',
  en:'“<b>Carotenoids</b> are the textbook’s third class of photosynthetic pigment, and it classes them as <b>isoprenoids, related to the steroids</b> — tetraterpenes whose colour comes from a long run of <b>three to eleven conjugated double bonds</b>. In the thylakoid they are <b>accessory pigments</b>: the book’s words are that the non-chlorophyll pigments <b>capture quanta of the incident radiation and transfer the energy of their excited state onto chlorophyll a in the reaction centre</b>, which <b>extends the spectrum of photosynthetically effective radiation</b>. Their second job is protective: the textbook says carotenoids <b>bind the active forms of oxygen — singlet oxygen, the superoxide anion radical and the hydroperoxide radical — and so act as antioxidants</b>. The most widespread one in nature is <b>β-carotene</b>; the oxidised derivatives are the <b>xanthophylls</b>, and it names <b>zeaxanthin</b> as the typical yellow pigment of the leaves of higher plants, and <b>lutein</b> in its figure.”',
  note:'两个作用分在两章：<b>辅助</b>在 <span class="pg">p205 p206</span>（§9.1.1），<b>抗氧化</b>在 <span class="pg">p187</span>（脂那一章，§8.2.4），书在那里自己回指 kap. 9.1.1。<b>第三类色素别忘了 fykobiliny</b>（开链的四吡咯，跟胆色素 biliverdin／bilirubin 同类），书把它排在第 2 类，<b>只在蓝细菌和红藻里</b>。<br><b>「protective pigment」这个说法在英文教材里到处都是，但它在这本书里的出处在脂那一章</b>——能指出这一点，等于告诉他你读的是这本书。<span class="pg">p186 p187 p205</span>'},

 {g:'光反应 · 按顺序走一遍', gn:'<b>题面要的是 process，所以这一组必须按顺序说，而且每个载体都要点名</b>——不许出现「the carrier」「the complex」。顺序就一条线：<b>水 → PS II → 质体醌 → cyt b₆f → 质体蓝素 → PS I → 铁氧还蛋白 → NADP⁺</b>。'},
 {n:'10', t:'光捕获：天线与反应中心',
  big:'绝大多数叶绿素<b>不做化学</b>，只做<b>天线</b>：吸光、把<b>能量</b>（不是电子）递进去。<b>总叶绿素 : 反应中心里的叶绿素 ≈ 200 : 1</b>。真正被氧化的只有反应中心那几个',
  en:'“Light is not absorbed where the chemistry happens. Most of the chlorophyll — the textbook gives the ratio of <b>total chlorophyll to chlorophyll in the reaction centre as about two hundred to one</b> — sits in <b>light-harvesting antenna systems</b> that are <b>not oxidised</b>; they only pass the absorbed energy inward, and the book stresses that <b>no charge separation occurs during that transfer</b>. The <b>reaction centre</b> is a few chlorophyll molecules bound to protein, and the book says the peculiarity of those molecules is that <b>because of their microenvironment in the pigment-protein complex they are capable of photo-oxidation</b> — of giving up an electron. Only two steps are written as light-dependent: <b>P plus a photon gives P-star</b>, and then the excited centre hands an electron to the <b>primary acceptor A-zero</b>, giving P-plus. The book says this is the <b>only reaction that is, strictly speaking, dependent on light</b>.”',
  good:'他问「为什么要天线」：“Because a single chlorophyll absorbs a photon only rarely, and the reaction centre is expensive protein. Two hundred cheap antenna molecules feed one centre and keep it working — the book adds that the high quantum yield means <b>this route of de-excitation is strongly preferred over the alternatives, fluorescence or conversion into heat</b>.”',
  note:'比值 ≈ 200 和 P + hν → P* 都<b>已回 400 dpi 核过</b>。<b>「只有这一步直接依赖光」这句话是整张卡的枢纽</b>——它同时解释了为什么其余全部（包括固定 CO₂）都叫「不直接需要光」。<span class="pg">p207 p208</span>'},
 {n:'11', t:'光系统 II · P680 · 水的光解',
  big:'<b>PS II ＝ 把水光氧化、把电子交给质体醌的那个光系统</b>（书的定义）。反应中心色素 <b>P680</b>（680 nm 是它的吸收峰）。补电子的来源是<b>水</b>：<b>2 H₂O → 4 H⁺ ＋ O₂ ＋ 4 e⁻</b>，干这件事的是 <b>OEC，oxygen-evolving complex</b>，在<b>类囊体内表面</b>',
  en:'“The textbook defines the two photosystems by what they do. <b>Photosystem II is the one that photo-oxidises water molecules and passes the electrons to plastoquinone.</b> Its reaction-centre pigment is <b>P680</b> — the P is for pigment and the number is the wavelength of its absorption maximum in nanometres; the same convention gives <b>P700</b> in photosystem I and <b>P870</b> in bacteria. When P680 gives its electron away it becomes a powerful oxidant, and the electron that fills the gap is taken <b>from water</b>. The book says <b>photolysis of water takes place at the inner surface of the thylakoid</b>, and its figure writes <b>two waters giving four protons and one oxygen molecule</b>, released into the thylakoid lumen. The catalyst is a <b>specific set of non-pigmented proteins responsible for the oxygen-evolving reactions</b> — the <b>oxygen-evolving complex, OEC</b>. The first acceptor inside photosystem II is <b>pheophytin</b>, chlorophyll without its magnesium, and from there the electron goes to the bound quinones <b>Q-A and Q-B</b>.”',
  warn:'⚠ <b>OEC 的锰簇书里一个字都没有</b>（grep「mangan」0 命中）。要说就明说是 general：“The cluster that does it is four manganese ions with a calcium, cycling through five oxidation states — that is general knowledge, this textbook only calls it a set of non-pigmented proteins.”',
  note:'<b>这一格答好了，「氧从哪来」和「为什么两个水」一起答完。</b>P680 / P700 / P870、Pheo、Q_A、Q_B、OEC 全部<b>已回 400 dpi 从 Obr. 9.5 和图注核过</b>（OCR 把下标全吃了）。<b>放电的方向</b>：质子进<b>内腔</b>，所以光解本身就在给梯度充电——<b>这是「表观质子转移」的一半</b>，见点 14。<span class="pg">p206 p207 p208</span>'},
 {n:'12', t:'从质体醌到 NADPH：每个载体点名',
  big:'<b>PQ（质体醌）</b>→ <b>cyt b₆f</b> → <b>PC（质体蓝素）</b>→ <b>PS I / P700</b> → <b>A₀ → A₁ → FeS</b> → <b>Fd（铁氧还蛋白）</b>→ <b>FNR（Fd–NADP⁺ 还原酶，带 FAD）</b>→ <b>NADPH</b>',
  en:'“From photosystem II the electron goes to <b>plastoquinone</b>, a small lipid-soluble quinone that moves <b>inside</b> the membrane carrying two electrons together with two protons — the book says it is the plant counterpart of coenzyme Q in the respiratory chain. It delivers them to the <b>cytochrome b6f complex</b>, which the book names as a cytochrome b and f complex with an iron–sulfur protein. From there a small <b>copper</b> protein, <b>plastocyanin</b>, carries one electron at a time along the <b>inner</b> face of the membrane to <b>photosystem I</b>. A second photon re-excites <b>P700</b>, and the electron passes through the acceptors <b>A-zero and A-one</b> and an <b>iron–sulfur centre</b> to <b>ferredoxin</b>, a small non-heme iron–sulfur protein on the stroma side. Finally <b>ferredoxin–NADP-plus reductase</b>, a flavoprotein with <b>FAD</b>, puts two electrons and a proton onto <b>NADP-plus</b> to give <b>NADPH</b>. The book says NADP-plus is <b>reduced on the outer side of the thylakoid</b> — the stroma side, which is exactly where it is needed.”',
  good:'他问 “Why plastocyanin and not a cytochrome?”：“The book gives plastocyanin its parameters rather than a reason: a protein of ten to twenty kilodaltons, a broad absorption maximum around <b>597 nanometres</b>, a standard potential at pH 7 of <b>370 millivolts</b>, containing <b>Cu²⁺</b>, and blue. Functionally it does what cytochrome c does in the mitochondrion: a small one-electron carrier sliding along the membrane face — only it uses copper instead of iron.”',
  note:'<b>书自己把这条链跟呼吸链并排写了</b>（<span class="pg">p206</span>）：类囊体膜里的载体<b>跟内膜同类</b>——醌、细胞色素、Fe-S 蛋白、黄素蛋白，外加 NADP。<b>plastocyanin 和 OEC 是植物独有的两样</b>，书专门点出来。plastocyanin 的四个参数<b>已回 400 dpi 核过</b>；<b>Fd–NADP⁺ reduktasa ＋ FAD 印在 Obr. 9.5 上</b>（正文没有，OCR 也没读出来）。ferredoxin 的定义在 <span class="pg">p046</span>：铁靠<b>半胱氨酸的 –SH 和游离硫离子</b>结合，<b>不是血红素</b>。<span class="pg">p045 p046 p206 p207 p208</span>'},
 {n:'13', t:'Z 图 —— 怎么画，两端的电位',
  big:'<b>两次光反应串联</b>：电子从 <b>H₂O（E°′ = +820 mV）</b>被推到 <b>NADP⁺（E°′ = −320 mV）</b>——<b>整个是上坡，1,14 V</b>，靠两个光子分两段推。横过来像个 <b>Z</b>',
  en:'“The Z scheme is how the textbook draws linear electron flow. Plot redox potential, oxidants at the bottom and reductants at the top: <b>the oxygen–water couple at plus 820 millivolts</b>, <b>NADP-plus over NADPH at minus 320</b>. Electrons have to go from the first to the second, which is <b>uphill by 1.14 volts</b> — it cannot happen on its own, and that is what the photons are for. The two <b>vertical</b> jumps are the two photochemical reactions in the reaction centres, which the book calls the <b>endergonic</b> steps, the ones requiring an input of energy as photons; the <b>downhill slopes</b> between them are the carrier chains, which it draws as a dashed line and calls <b>exergonic</b>. Two up-strokes with a fall in between look like a letter Z lying on its side. The book says the same scheme can be drawn on a scale of Gibbs energy instead.”',
  note:'<b>他可能让你画 Z 图。六步，边画边说</b>：① 画一条<b>竖轴</b>，下端写 <b>oxidants / +</b>，上端写 <b>reductants / −</b>，轴名写 <b>E°′</b>；② 左下角点一个点，标 <b>H₂O, +820 mV</b>；③ 从它<b>向上</b>画一根竖箭头到高处，箭头旁写 <b>hν</b>，落点标 <b>P680*</b>——这是 <b>PS II</b>；④ 从落点画一条<b>向下向右的虚线</b>，中途标上 <b>PQ → cyt b₆f → PC</b>，落到中间高度，标 <b>P700</b>；⑤ 再画一根<b>向上</b>的竖箭头，旁边写 <b>hν</b>，落点标 <b>P700*</b>——这是 <b>PS I</b>；⑥ 从那里画<b>向下向右</b>的虚线，标 <b>A₀ → A₁ → FeS → Fd → FNR</b>，终点标 <b>NADP⁺, −320 mV</b>。<b>画完把笔放下说一句</b>：“the two vertical arrows are the two photons; everything else runs downhill.”<br>820 mV 和 −320 mV <b>已回 400 dpi 核过</b>（OCR 把 820 读成 520）；820 跟书自己 Tab. 6.2 里氧的 <b>+0,82 V</b> 对得上（<span class="pg">p135</span>），所以这是<b>书内部自洽</b>的，不是我们推的。1,14 V 是两端相减，跟卡 <b>25</b> 呼吸链走的<b>是同一段电位</b>——只是方向相反。<span class="pg">p135 p207 p208</span>'},
 {n:'14', t:'质子梯度：方向、怎么形成、ATP 合酶',
  big:'质子<b>从基质往类囊体内腔里进</b>（跟线粒体<b>正好相反</b>）。两个来源：<b>光解</b>在内腔放质子，<b>质体醌</b>真的把质子背过膜。合酶 <b>CF₀CF₁</b> 的头<b>朝基质</b>，质子<b>从里往外</b>流过它 → ATP。书的图上：<b>3 个质子换 1 个 ATP</b>',
  en:'“The textbook separates two ways electrons move protons, in the same words it used for the respiratory chain. The transfer is <b>apparent</b> when protons are simply consumed on one side and released on the other — here, <b>released inside by the photolysis of water and consumed outside by the reduction of NADP-plus</b>. It is <b>real</b> when protons are physically carried across, and it says the real transfer is done by the <b>hydrophobic plastoquinone molecules operating together with the cytochrome b6f complex inside the membrane</b>. Either way, on illumination the proton concentration <b>rises throughout the inside of the thylakoid</b> and falls at the outer surface: a transmembrane difference in H-plus. <b>ATP synthase</b> — the last supramolecular complex of the thylakoid, built of non-pigmented protein only, and the book says its composition and function are <b>again analogous to the ATP synthase of the inner mitochondrial membrane</b> — lets the protons back out and makes ATP from ADP and phosphate. Its two parts are called <b>CF-zero</b>, in the membrane, and <b>CF-one</b>, the catalytic head. The figure marks <b>three protons per ATP</b>.”',
  warn:'<b>方向是这道题最容易说反的一句。</b>线粒体：质子被泵<b>出</b>基质，合酶的头<b>朝基质</b>，质子<b>回到</b>基质。类囊体：质子被搬<b>进</b>内腔，合酶的头<b>朝基质（膜外）</b>，质子<b>从内腔往外</b>流。<b>书自己把这句话写死了</b>：<i>Obrácená je však orientace thylakoidních membrán</i>——类囊体膜的取向是<b>反</b>的。',
  good:'他问 “Why are they the other way round?”：“The textbook answers this one — it says the explanation of the opposite orientation lies in the <b>evolution of the membranes and the different ways they pinched off and closed on themselves</b>. So it is a question about how the organelle was formed, not about the chemistry.”',
  note:'<b>这一格就是卡 25 的镜像格</b>，两张卡要一起背。卡 25 的陷阱题 “Which way do the protons go?” 已经带了半句类囊体的方向。<b>3 H⁺/ATP 是 Obr. 9.5 上印的</b>（已回 400 dpi 核过）；图上还标了 <b>8 H⁺ ＋ 4 QH₂</b> 在 cyt b₆f 那一段，但它跟同一张图上的 <b>2 H₂O → 4 H⁺ ＋ O₂</b> 不在同一个计量尺度上（四个电子只够还原两个质体醌），<b>所以被追数字时只引 3 H⁺/ATP 和 2 H₂O → O₂ 这两条，别引 8 和 4。</b><span class="pg">p208 p209</span>'},
 {n:'15', t:'环式光合磷酸化',
  big:'电子<b>不去 NADP⁺，绕回反应中心</b>：<b>Fd → 质体醌 → cyt b₆f → PC → P700</b>。<b>只有 PS I 参加</b>，<b>不放氧、不产 NADPH</b>，书的原话是<b>「唯一的结果是产生 ATP」</b>',
  en:'“If the electrons <b>return to the reaction centre</b> instead of going on to NADP-plus, the textbook calls it <b>cyclic electron flow</b>, or <b>cyclic photophosphorylation</b>, and its sentence is that <b>its only result is the formation of ATP</b>. It says the cycle runs between <b>ferredoxin and the plastoquinones</b>, and that <b>photosystem I is the one that takes part in it</b> — so no water is split, no oxygen is evolved and no NADPH is made; the electron simply goes round and pumps protons on the way. The linear route, two photoreactions and two photosystems in series, is the <b>non-cyclic</b> one. What it is <b>for</b> is the ATP-to-NADPH balance: fixing CO₂ needs more ATP per NADPH than linear flow delivers, and the cycle makes up the difference — I should say that the reason is general, the textbook gives the mechanism without the purpose.”',
  warn:'<b>「为什么要有环式」书里没写。</b>只写了「唯一的结果是 ATP」。所以那个理由（3 ATP : 2 NADPH 的比例对不上）要标 general。',
  note:'<b>「cyclic / non-cyclic」这一对是很典型的追问</b>，而答案的形状很简单：<b>非环式＝两个光系统串联＝ATP ＋ NADPH ＋ O₂；环式＝只有 PS I ＋ 绕回去＝只有 ATP</b>。<span class="pg">p208</span>'},

 {g:'固定 CO₂ · Calvin 循环', gn:'第二半。<b>三个阶段：羧化 → 还原 → 再生。</b>每个阶段只记「谁变成谁 ＋ 那个酶 ＋ 花掉什么」，中间那十几个磷酸糖不用背——<b>书自己说再生段走的就是戊糖循环里那几类反应</b>。'},
 {n:'16', t:'第一步 ＋ rubisco 的全名',
  big:'<b>CO₂ ＋ RuBP（五碳）→ 两个 3-phosphoglycerate（三碳）</b>。酶是 <b>ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase，RubisCO</b>。<b>第一个稳定产物是三个碳的 3-PG——C-3 植物就是这么得名的</b>',
  en:'“The first reaction of the Calvin cycle is the capture of inorganic CO₂ and its incorporation into an organic molecule, <b>ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate</b> — a five-carbon sugar carrying a phosphate at each end. Chemically it is a <b>carboxylation</b>, and the enzyme is <b>ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase</b>, RubisCO. The textbook’s mechanism: each active site converts RuBP into an <b>enediolate ion</b>, which is reactive enough to bind CO₂; that gives a <b>six-carbon intermediate which hydrolyses very quickly into two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate</b>. So the <b>first stable product of CO₂ fixation is 3-phosphoglycerate, a three-carbon compound</b> — and the book adds that this is why plants using the Calvin cycle are called <b>C-3 plants</b>. The enzyme is <b>soluble, sits in the stroma</b>, and the book calls it <b>one of the most widespread macromolecular proteins on Earth</b>. It has two kinds of subunit, <b>small and large</b>, and the functional enzyme is a dimer of units of four small and four large — eight and eight, with <b>eight active sites</b>.”',
  warn:'<b>全名要能一口气说出来</b>，而且要说清 <b>/oxygenase</b> 那一半是<b>哪儿来的</b>：书在光呼吸那一节才解释——<i>RubisCO（除羧化酶活性外还有加氧酶活性——酶的全名由此而来）</i>。<b>先说全名，再说「后半个名字要等讲到光呼吸才有意义」，这是一个很漂亮的自留口子。</b>',
  note:'<b>「最丰富的蛋白」要小心。</b>书写的是「<b>之一</b>」（jeden z nejrozšířenějších）。通用说法直接说「地球上最丰富的蛋白」——<b>报的时候用书的措辞，被追问再说 “it is usually quoted as the single most abundant protein on Earth, which is general rather than this book”。</b><span class="pg">p209 p210 p212</span>'},
 {n:'17', t:'三个阶段 ＋ 怎么画这个环',
  big:'<b>① 羧化</b>：3 CO₂ ＋ 3 RuBP → 6 × 3-PG。<b>② 还原</b>：3-PG →(ATP)→ 1,3-BPG →(NADPH)→ <b>GAP</b>，<b>就是糖酵解那两步倒着走</b>。<b>③ 再生</b>：五个 GAP 重排回三个 Ru5P，再<b>花一个 ATP</b> 磷酸化成 RuBP。<b>净产物：书说是一个 F6P</b>',
  en:'“Three phases. <b>Carboxylation</b>, which I have just described. <b>Reduction</b>: 3-phosphoglycerate is converted in two steps, <b>spending ATP and NADPH</b>, into <b>glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate</b> — and the textbook points out that the two enzymes, <b>phosphoglycerate kinase and glyceraldehyde-phosphate dehydrogenase, are known from glycolysis, where the same steps run in the opposite direction</b>. <b>Regeneration</b>: glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate is the key compound of the cycle, and the book says it is processed by four possible routes which are always only <b>isomerisation — that is, aldose to ketose — epimerisation, dephosphorylation and phosphorylation of phosphorylated sugars</b>, the same kinds of reaction as in the <b>pentose phosphate pathway</b>; the end point is always <b>ribulose-5-phosphate</b>, which an <b>ATP-dependent kinase</b> phosphorylates back to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, closing the cycle. The textbook gives the <b>net yield of the cycle as one fructose-6-phosphate</b>, which goes on through glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, to cellulose or starch, or into glycolysis.”',
  good:'他问哪些酶：“The book’s scheme names them: ribulose-5-phosphate kinase, ribulose-bisphosphate carboxylase, phosphoglycerate kinase, glyceraldehyde-phosphate dehydrogenase, triose-phosphate isomerase, aldolase, fructose-bisphosphatase, transketolase, sedoheptulose-1,7-bisphosphatase, ribose-phosphate isomerase and phosphopentose epimerase.”',
  note:'<b>他可能让你画这个环。五步，边画边说，不要画成十二个中间产物</b>：① 画一个圆，<b>右上角</b>写 <b>RuBP (5C)</b>；② 一支箭头从外面指进来标 <b>CO₂</b>，落点写 <b>2 × 3-PG (3C)</b>，箭上写 <b>rubisco</b>；③ 沿圆往下，箭上依次写 <b>ATP</b> 和 <b>NADPH</b>，落点写 <b>GAP (3C)</b>；④ 从 GAP 引一支箭<b>出圆</b>，写 <b>F6P → glucose / starch / cellulose</b>——<b>这是唯一离开循环的那条支</b>；⑤ 圆的左半边一条箭从 GAP 绕回 RuBP，中间写 <b>rearrangement（pentose phosphate reactions）</b>，箭上写 <b>ATP</b>。<b>画完说一句</b>：“three carbons in as CO₂, three carbons out as triose; everything else on the circle is bookkeeping to get the acceptor back.”<br><b>「还原段就是糖酵解倒着走」这半句是书自己说的</b>，是全卡最省事的一条——她糖酵解学得好，这里可以直接借力。<span class="pg">p210 p211 p212</span>'},
 {n:'18', t:'光呼吸 —— rubisco 名字的后半截（桥到卡 28）',
  big:'rubisco 也能把 <b>O₂</b> 接到 RuBP 上：<b>RuBP ＋ O₂ → 3-PG ＋ phosphoglycolate</b>，白白浪费一个碳。它<b>跟 Calvin 循环抢同一个底物</b>。书说它<b>可能是保护</b>（CO₂ 不够时防止光氧化），也是<b>植物合成甘氨酸和丝氨酸的路</b>',
  en:'“The oxygenase half of the name is <b>photorespiration</b>. The textbook says most plants, while illuminated, also <b>consume</b> oxygen, by a process quite different from mitochondrial respiration, and that it involves three enzymes: <b>RubisCO, phosphoglycolate phosphatase and glycolate oxygenase</b>. RubisCO oxygenates ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, which breaks into <b>3-phosphoglycerate and phosphoglycolate</b>; the phosphoglycolate is hydrolysed to <b>glycolate</b> and oxidised to <b>glyoxylate</b>. The book is honest that <b>the reasons are not yet entirely clear</b> and that the sequence <b>seems completely useless for the plant, and is competitive with the Calvin cycle because it uses the same substrate</b>. It offers two functions: it <b>probably protects the photosynthetic apparatus against harmful photo-oxidation when there is not enough CO₂</b>, and it is a <b>route of plant biosynthesis of glycine and serine</b>. It runs across three compartments — <b>chloroplast, peroxisome and mitochondrion</b>.”',
  good:'<b>桥到 #28，一句就够</b>：“And this is exactly the pressure the C-4 and CAM plants are built against: they fix CO₂ first with <b>PEP carboxylase</b>, which the textbook says works at <b>lower CO₂ concentrations — a lower Km</b> — than RubisCO needs, and then release it again around RubisCO. The book’s phrase is that the C-4 mechanism <b>concentrates CO₂</b> for the carboxylase.”',
  note:'<b>竞争性这一点值钱</b>：同一个活性位点、同一个底物 RuBP，O₂ 和 CO₂ 抢——<b>这正好是卡 19（酶抑制类型）里「竞争性」的一个真实例子</b>，可以顺手接过去。C-4 在 <span class="pg">p212</span>（Hatch 和 Slack，1970），<b>属于卡 28，这里只碰一下不展开</b>。<span class="pg">p212</span>'},

 {g:'对照与陷阱', gn:'<b>这两格决定他觉得你是背过还是懂了。</b>对照那一格他几乎一定会问，因为它是检验「你分不分得清两台机器」的最短路径。'},
 {n:'19', t:'光合 vs 呼吸：镜像，但不是逆反应',
  big:'总式看上去正好相反（<b>+2867</b> vs <b>−2870</b>），但<b>不是同一条路倒着走</b>：区室不同、载体不同（<b>NADPH</b> vs <b>NADH</b>）、梯度方向不同、驱动力不同（<b>光子</b> vs <b>底物的电位落差</b>）',
  en:'“They look like each other’s reverse and they are not the same reaction run backwards. Four differences. <b>Compartment</b>: photosynthesis is in the chloroplast — thylakoid membrane and stroma — respiration in the mitochondrion, inner membrane and matrix. <b>Carrier</b>: photosynthesis reduces <b>NADP-plus</b>, respiration oxidises <b>NADH</b>; this textbook keeps them strictly apart, saying the <b>NADPH is not oxidised in the respiratory chain but used for synthetic reactions</b>, and that the hydrogen donor for biosynthesis is NADPH while oxidation produces NADH. <b>Direction of the gradient</b>: in the mitochondrion protons are pumped <b>out</b> of the matrix and return through the synthase; in the chloroplast they are driven <b>into</b> the thylakoid and leave through a synthase whose head faces the other way. <b>Driving force</b>: in the respiratory chain electrons fall from minus 0.32 volts to plus 0.82 on their own; in photosynthesis they are pushed the whole way <b>uphill</b>, and two photons pay for it. What is the same is the principle — carriers in a membrane, a proton gradient, and an ATP synthase the book says is analogous in composition and function.”',
  note:'<b>「一个磷酸就把两条路分开了」</b>——NADPH 和 NADH 只差核糖上一个磷酸，细胞却拿它们做完全不同的事。这句话在卡 <b>25</b> 的陷阱格里已经有了，两边说的是同一件事。<br>植物<b>两台机器都有</b>，而且同时开：叶绿体造糖，线粒体烧糖。他要是问「那植物晚上做什么」——<b>呼吸，跟你一样</b>。<span class="pg">p206 p208 p219 p220</span>'},
 {n:'20', t:'五条陷阱',
  big:'① 氧来自<b>水</b>，不是 CO₂ · ② 暗反应<b>不是夜里</b>跑的 · ③ 光反应<b>不固定碳</b> · ④ 叶绿素<b>不被消耗</b>，它是催化剂不是底物 · ⑤ ATP 合酶跟线粒体里的<b>是同一台机器</b>，只是装反了',
  en:'“Five things that are easy to say wrong. <b>One</b>: the oxygen comes from <b>water</b>, and the textbook writes twelve waters rather than six precisely to make that point. <b>Two</b>: the dark reactions are not nocturnal — the book itself calls the name inappropriate, because it suggests the conversion does not happen in the light; it runs whenever ATP and NADPH are available, which in practice is in daylight. <b>Three</b>: the light reactions fix <b>no carbon at all</b>; their products are ATP, NADPH and oxygen, and carbon enters only at RubisCO. <b>Four</b>: chlorophyll is <b>not consumed</b>. It gives up an electron and gets one back within microseconds, so it turns over like a catalyst; that is why a leaf does not run out of it. <b>Five</b>: the ATP synthase is the <b>same machine</b> as in the mitochondrion — the book says composition and function are analogous, CF-zero and CF-one against F-zero and F-one — only the membrane is oriented the other way round, so protons enter the thylakoid and leave through the synthase into the stroma.”',
  warn:'<b>第三条是最常掉的分。</b>说 “the light reactions produce sugar” 就等于承认没分清两半。<b>光反应产 ATP、NADPH、O₂；糖只在基质里出现。</b>',
  good:'第六条，顺势可加（general）：“And the oxygen is not for the plant’s own benefit — the textbook’s evolution chapter says that when photosynthesis switched from hydrogen sulfide to water, the oxygen released into a reducing atmosphere <b>acted at first as a dangerous poison</b>, and only the organisms that learned to pass electrons onto it survived.”（<span class="pg">p019</span>，约 25–30 亿年前）'}
],
end:'<b>一句话记住整张卡：光只干一件事——把电子从水里拽出来、推到 NADP⁺ 上；顺路造一个质子梯度换成 ATP。然后基质里的 rubisco 拿这两样把 CO₂ 接到一个五碳糖上。</b>产氧的是<b>水的光解</b>，固碳的是<b>Calvin 循环</b>，两者<b>只通过 ATP 和 NADPH 连着</b>——这跟呼吸链和合酶只通过质子梯度连着，是同一种设计。被问数字先给书的：<b>12 H₂O ＋ 6 CO₂ → C₆H₁₂O₆ ＋ 6 O₂ ＋ 6 H₂O，ΔG°′ = +2867 kJ/mol</b>。',

/* ---------------- 演练 ---------------- */
sib:[
['<b>定义</b>：把<b>光能变成化学能</b>；光同时驱动<b>水的氧化</b>和 <b>CO₂ 的还原</b>',
 '“The biological conversion of radiant energy into chemical energy — light drives the oxidation of a donor, in green plants water, and the reduction of carbon dioxide to carbohydrate.”',
 '书 §9.1 的原话。通式 H₂D + A → H₂A + D，<b>A 永远是 CO₂</b>。<span class="pg">p202</span>'],
['<b>总式</b>：<b>12 H₂O ＋ 6 CO₂ → C₆H₁₂O₆ ＋ 6 O₂ ＋ 6 H₂O</b>，<b>ΔG°′ = +2867</b>；<b>氧来自水</b>',
 '“Twelve waters and six CO₂ give a hexose, six oxygens and six waters, at plus 2867 kilojoules per mole. The textbook writes twelve rather than six to stress that the oxygen comes from water and not from carbon dioxide.”',
 '<b>正号</b>——吸能，所以要光。镜像：葡萄糖完全氧化 <b>−2870</b>。<span class="pg">p135 p203</span>'],
['<b>两半 ＋ 地点</b>：光反应在<b>类囊体膜</b>（出 ATP ＋ NADPH ＋ O₂），固定 CO₂ 在<b>基质</b>（花掉它们）',
 '“The light reactions run in the thylakoid membrane and give ATP, NADPH and oxygen; the fixation of CO₂ runs in the stroma and spends the ATP and NADPH.”',
 '「dark」＝<b>不直接需要光</b>。<b>书自己说这个名字不合适。</b><span class="pg">p209</span>'],
['<b>账</b>：每 CO₂ 花 <b>3 ATP ＋ 2 NADPH</b>（通用数）；<b>六圈</b>一个己糖 → <b>18 ATP ＋ 12 NADPH</b>',
 '“Three ATP and two NADPH per CO₂, so eighteen and twelve for one hexose — those are the standard figures; this textbook prints no ATP count, only the overall equation.”',
 '<b>标 general。</b>书印的数只有 3 H⁺/ATP 和 2 H₂O → 4 H⁺ ＋ O₂。'],
['<b>色素</b>：<b>卟啉 ＋ 中心 Mg ＋ 植醇尾</b>；<b>只有 chlorophyll a</b> 做能量转换，b 和类胡萝卜素是辅助',
 '“Chlorophyll is a porphyrin with magnesium at the centre and a twenty-carbon phytyl tail; the textbook says only chlorophyll a is the actual transformer of energy, and the rest are accessory pigments feeding it.”',
 '卟啉＝四吡咯＋<b>甲川桥</b>，跟血红素同一个环，金属不同。<span class="pg">p205</span>'],
['<b>为什么绿</b>：吃掉<b>蓝 415–440</b> 和<b>红 640–700</b>，中间<b>没被吸走</b>的就是绿',
 '“Chlorophyll absorbs in the blue and in the red; the green in between is what is not absorbed, so it is reflected — the textbook says the complementary colour of red is green, and that is why the plant world is green.”',
 '书还说蓝带<b>对光合不重要</b>（第二单重态寿命太短）。<span class="pg">p205</span>'],
['<b>天线</b>：<b>约 200 个叶绿素喂 1 个反应中心</b>；天线只传<b>能量</b>，不分离电荷',
 '“Total chlorophyll to chlorophyll in the reaction centre is about two hundred to one; the antenna pigments are not oxidised, they only pass the energy inward, with no charge separation.”',
 '<b>「只有 P + hν → P* 这一步直接依赖光」</b>——全卡的枢纽句。<span class="pg">p207</span>'],
['<b>光反应的顺序</b>：<b>H₂O → PS II(P680) → PQ → cyt b₆f → PC → PS I(P700) → Fd → FNR → NADPH</b>',
 '“Water to photosystem two at P680, then plastoquinone, the cytochrome b6f complex, plastocyanin, photosystem one at P700, ferredoxin, and ferredoxin–NADP reductase onto NADP-plus.”',
 '<b>每个载体都要点名</b>；书自己说这些载体跟线粒体内膜同类。<span class="pg">p206 p207 p208</span>'],
['<b>光解</b>：在<b>类囊体内表面</b>，<b>2 H₂O → 4 H⁺ ＋ O₂ ＋ 4 e⁻</b>，干这事的是 <b>OEC</b>',
 '“Photolysis of water happens at the inner surface of the thylakoid: two waters give four protons and one oxygen, catalysed by the oxygen-evolving complex.”',
 '<b>锰簇书里没有</b>，要说就标 general。<span class="pg">p206 p208</span>'],
['<b>梯度方向</b>：质子<b>进</b>类囊体内腔；合酶的头<b>朝基质</b>，质子<b>从里往外</b>流 → ATP',
 '“Protons are driven into the thylakoid and come back out through the synthase, whose knobs face the stroma — the opposite orientation from the mitochondrion, as the textbook says explicitly.”',
 '<b>说反就整台机器反了。</b>书给了理由：膜的进化和自我封闭方式。<span class="pg">p208 p209</span>'],
['<b>环式</b>：电子从 <b>Fd 绕回质体醌</b>，<b>只有 PS I</b>，<b>只产 ATP</b>，不放氧不产 NADPH',
 '“In cyclic flow the electrons return to the reaction centre by way of the plastoquinones; only photosystem one takes part, and the textbook says its only result is the formation of ATP.”',
 '「为什么要它」是 general（补 ATP:NADPH 的比例）。<span class="pg">p208</span>'],
['<b>Calvin 第一步</b>：<b>CO₂ ＋ RuBP → 2 × 3-PG</b>，酶是 <b>ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase</b>',
 '“Carbon dioxide is added to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate by ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase, and the six-carbon intermediate splits at once into two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate — the first stable product, and three carbons, hence C-3 plants.”',
 '全名一口气说完。<b>/oxygenase 那一半留到光呼吸再解释。</b><span class="pg">p209 p210</span>'],
['<b>三阶段</b>：<b>羧化 → 还原（ATP ＋ NADPH，糖酵解倒着走）→ 再生（戊糖那套 ＋ 1 个 ATP）</b>',
 '“Carboxylation, then reduction to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate by the two glycolytic enzymes running backwards, then regeneration of ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate through the pentose phosphate reactions and one more ATP.”',
 '书说净产物是<b>一个 F6P</b>，再走 G6P → 葡萄糖／纤维素／糖酵解。<span class="pg">p210 p211</span>'],
['<b>光呼吸</b>：rubisco 接 <b>O₂</b>，<b>抢同一个底物 RuBP</b>；书说<b>可能是保护</b>，也是 <b>glycine／serine</b> 的来路',
 '“RubisCO also oxygenates ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, giving 3-phosphoglycerate and phosphoglycolate; it competes with the Calvin cycle for the same substrate, and the textbook says it probably protects the apparatus from photo-oxidation when CO₂ is short.”',
 '<b>这是通往 #28（C-4 / CAM）的桥。</b><span class="pg">p212</span>'],
['<b>陷阱</b>：氧来自<b>水</b> · 暗反应<b>不是夜里</b> · 光反应<b>不固碳</b> · 叶绿素<b>不被消耗</b> · 合酶跟线粒体<b>同一台</b>',
 '“The oxygen comes from water; the dark reactions are not nocturnal; the light reactions fix no carbon at all; chlorophyll is not consumed but turns over like a catalyst; and the ATP synthase is the same machine as the mitochondrial one, mounted the other way round.”',
 '五条几乎每次都被问，<b>都是一句话说错就掉分的那种</b>。']
],

segs:[
 {tag:'段 1 · 定义', h:'先定义，再总式，再拆成两半 —— 三句就把题面交代完',
  p:['“<b>Photosynthesis is the biological conversion of radiant energy into chemical energy</b> — that is this textbook’s own definition. Light drives two things at the same time: the <b>oxidation of a donor</b> and the <b>reduction of carbon dioxide</b> to carbohydrate. The textbook writes the general form as H₂D plus A, over the pigments, giving H₂A plus D, where H₂D is the donor of electrons and protons and A the acceptor — and for every photosynthesising organism the acceptor is carbon dioxide. In green plants the donor is <b>water</b>, which is why oxygen is released; some bacteria use hydrogen sulfide and deposit sulfur instead.',
     'The overall equation, in the book’s own form: <b>twelve waters plus six CO₂ give one hexose, six oxygen molecules and six waters</b>, with a standard Gibbs energy of <b>plus 2867 kilojoules per mole</b>. The plus sign is the whole point — the reaction is endergonic, and the light is what pays for it. The book writes twelve waters rather than six deliberately, <b>to stress that the six oxygen molecules come from water and not from carbon dioxide</b>.',
     'The <b>process</b> then divides into two halves in two places. The <b>light reactions</b> run in the <b>thylakoid membrane</b>, a stack of closed flattened sacs inside the chloroplast, and their first stable products are <b>ATP and NADPH</b>, with oxygen released as water is split. The <b>fixation and reduction of CO₂</b> runs in the <b>stroma</b>, the soluble phase around the thylakoids, and spends exactly those two. They are often called the dark reactions, and this textbook says the name is <b>not appropriate, because it suggests the conversion does not happen in the light</b> — it means not directly requiring light, and it runs for as long as ATP and NADPH are available.”'],
  note:'<b>三句话把题面上两个实词都交代完了</b>：<b>photosynthesis</b>（第一句给定义，第二句给式子）、<b>process</b>（第三句给形状——两半、两个地点、什么进什么出）。他嘴里多半只有 “What is photosynthesis?”，<b>剩下的结构要你自己接上去</b>。<br><b>「暗反应不是夜里」这一条放在段 1 而不是陷阱格</b>，因为它是一个几乎必被追的点，而且抢先说出来会显得你读过书。'},
 {tag:'段 2 · general', h:'账、地点、意义 —— 三行说完，别展开',
  p:['“The account, per carbon dioxide fixed: in go <b>one CO₂, one molecule of ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, three ATP and two NADPH</b>; out come <b>two molecules of three-carbon phosphoglycerate</b>, reduced to two triose phosphates, with the ADP, phosphate and NADP-plus handed back to the membrane. The acceptor, the ribulose bisphosphate, is <b>regenerated rather than consumed</b>, so it behaves like a catalyst. I should flag that the three and the two are the standard counts — <b>this textbook prints no ATP and NADPH figures</b>; what it prints is three protons per ATP at the synthase and two waters per oxygen at the other end.',
     'The net of it: the light reactions make <b>ATP and NADPH and nothing else that lasts</b>, and the Calvin cycle spends them on carbon. Six turns — six CO₂, eighteen ATP, twelve NADPH — give one hexose, and the book states the net yield of the cycle as <b>one fructose-6-phosphate</b>, which goes on to glucose-6-phosphate and then to glucose, starch, cellulose or straight into glycolysis. Its own warning is worth repeating: <b>the six carbons do not all end up in the same sugar molecule</b>, and hexose is not the point of the exercise — most of the fixed carbon goes into the central pathways, it names the citric acid cycle, as carbon skeletons for amino acids, fatty acids, nucleotides and pigments.',
     'So: <b>where</b> — thylakoid membrane for the light half, stroma for the carbon half, both inside the chloroplast. <b>Why it matters</b> — it is the step that puts solar energy into the biosphere, and every carbon in the sugar comes from CO₂, every oxygen molecule released from water.”'],
  note:'<b>三行账 ＋ 地点 ＋ 意义，正是代谢题的通用开口模板</b>（见 `DRILL.md`）。<br><b>「书没印 ATP 数」这句话必须说</b>——她一旦被追「书上第几页写的」，含糊就变成可以当场证伪的错误。说出来反而显得读得细。'},
 {tag:'段 3 · 稍展开', h:'光反应按顺序走一遍，然后 Calvin 三阶段',
  p:['“Light is absorbed mostly by <b>antenna</b> chlorophyll — about two hundred molecules for every one in a reaction centre — which is not oxidised and only passes the energy inward. In the <b>reaction centre</b>, a few chlorophylls bound to protein, one molecule can actually give up an electron; the book says that is the <b>only step strictly dependent on light</b>.',
     '<b>Photosystem II</b> is defined by the textbook as the one that <b>photo-oxidises water and passes the electrons to plastoquinone</b>. Its pigment is <b>P680</b>, named for its absorption maximum. The electron goes first to <b>pheophytin</b> — chlorophyll without its magnesium — and then to the bound quinones. The gap left in P680 is filled from <b>water</b>: at the <b>inner</b> surface of the thylakoid the <b>oxygen-evolving complex</b> splits two waters into <b>four protons, four electrons and one oxygen molecule</b>, and the protons stay inside.',
     '<b>Plastoquinone</b>, a lipid-soluble quinone — the plant counterpart of coenzyme Q — carries two electrons with two protons across inside the membrane to the <b>cytochrome b6f complex</b>, and this is where protons are <b>really</b> carried across rather than just released on one side. From there the blue copper protein <b>plastocyanin</b> takes one electron at a time along the inner face to <b>photosystem I</b>. A second photon re-excites <b>P700</b>; the electron passes through the acceptors A-zero and A-one and an iron–sulfur centre to <b>ferredoxin</b> on the stroma side, and <b>ferredoxin–NADP reductase</b>, a flavoprotein with FAD, reduces <b>NADP-plus to NADPH</b> — on the outside, which is where it is needed. Drawn against redox potential, the two photon-driven jumps and the two downhill runs between them make the letter <b>Z</b>: from the oxygen–water couple at <b>plus 820 millivolts</b> to NADP-plus at <b>minus 320</b>, uphill all the way.',
     'Meanwhile the protons accumulate <b>inside</b> the thylakoid, and <b>ATP synthase</b> — CF-zero in the membrane, CF-one as a head on the <b>stroma</b> side — lets them out and makes ATP. The book says its composition and function are <b>analogous to the mitochondrial one</b>, but the membrane is oriented the other way round. If the electrons return from <b>ferredoxin</b> to the plastoquinones instead of going to NADP-plus, that is <b>cyclic photophosphorylation</b>, in which only photosystem one takes part, and the book says <b>its only result is ATP</b>.',
     'Then the stroma. <b>Carboxylation</b>: <b>ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase</b> adds CO₂ to a five-carbon sugar bisphosphate; the six-carbon intermediate hydrolyses at once into <b>two three-carbon 3-phosphoglycerates</b> — the first stable product, which is why these are C-3 plants. <b>Reduction</b>: two steps spending ATP and NADPH to <b>glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate</b>, the book noting that both enzymes are the glycolytic ones running backwards. <b>Regeneration</b>: the triose phosphates are rearranged by the reactions of the pentose phosphate pathway back to <b>ribulose-5-phosphate</b>, and one ATP phosphorylates it to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate, closing the cycle.”'],
  note:'<b>时间不够就砍到四句</b>：水 → PS II → 质体醌 → cyt b₆f → 质体蓝素 → PS I → 铁氧还蛋白 → NADPH；质子进内腔，合酶放出来；rubisco 把 CO₂ 接到 RuBP 上出两个 3-PG；ATP 和 NADPH 把它还原成三碳糖，剩下的都是再生。<br><b>不要砍「书自己定义 PS II 是干什么的」和「只有一步依赖光」</b>——那是他追问时的落点。'},
 {tag:'段 4 · 留口子', h:'诚实地接回主场：rubisco 是蛋白质工程改了五十年没改动的那个靶',
  p:['“This chapter is a long way from what I work on, so let me connect it where the connection is real — and it is, because <b>rubisco is the classic hard target of protein engineering</b>. The textbook already gives the reason in passing: it calls it <b>one of the most widespread macromolecular proteins on Earth</b>, and it says the enzyme has <b>two activities in the same active site</b> — carboxylase and oxygenase, which is why the full name is ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase. A plant makes so much of it precisely because each copy works slowly and wastes some of its turnovers on oxygen.',
     'That makes it the obvious thing to improve, and it has resisted for decades. The reason is a <b>trade-off</b>: across natural rubiscos, the variants that discriminate better between CO₂ and O₂ tend to be the slower ones — selectivity and rate are coupled, so a library screen that selects for one gives back the other. I should say that this trade-off is <b>general knowledge and not in this textbook</b>, which only gives the two activities and the competition for the same substrate.',
     'That shape is familiar from my own work. I do <b>directed evolution</b> — building libraries of protein variants and selecting them by <b>yeast display</b>, where each yeast cell shows one variant on its surface and is sorted by how tightly it binds. What you learn very quickly is that <b>you get what you select for, including the thing you did not mean to select for</b>: if the selection reports only on affinity, you can improve affinity and lose expression, or stability, or specificity. Rubisco is the textbook case of a protein where the property you want and the property you can measure are pulling in opposite directions — and my answer to that in the lab is the same one people use here: <b>select on both axes at once, or you simply walk along the trade-off instead of off it</b>.”'],
  note:'<b>这一段是诚实的，不是硬接。</b>她是做定向进化和酵母展示的，跟植物生化八竿子打不着——所以桥不能架在「我也研究光合」上，只能架在<b>「这是一个我认得的蛋白质工程问题」</b>上，而且要短。<br><b>两个必须标 general 的地方</b>：「速率 vs 选择性的权衡」和「最丰富的蛋白」这个最高级——书写的是「之一」。<b>不点具体病原体、不展开课题</b>（见 `DRILL.md` 的三条机制）。<br>口子落在<b>横向卡 fitness landscape（#7）</b>上：他顺着问「那你怎么选」，就走进她的主场。'}
],

why:{
 rungs:[
  ['为什么非要有<b>两个</b>光系统？一个不行吗？','因为要爬的坡太高。书自己给了两端：<b>H₂O/O₂ 是 +820 mV，NADP⁺/NADPH 是 −320 mV</b>（<span class="pg">p208</span>，已回 400 dpi 核过），也就是要把电子<b>逆着 1,14 V 往上推</b>。一个可见光光子的能量不够一次推完，所以书把它画成 <b>Z</b>：<b>两次光反应串联</b>，中间让电子先沿着载体链滑下来一段再第二次被推上去。英文：“Because the whole span from water to NADP-plus is 1.14 volts uphill, and one visible photon will not do it in a single step — so the textbook draws two photoreactions in series, with a downhill run between them.”'],
  ['那为什么中间要先<b>滑下来</b>一段？直接推两次不行吗？','因为那段下坡<b>不是浪费，是在挣 ATP</b>。滑下来的那一段落在<b>质体醌和 cyt b₆f</b> 上，它们把电子的落差换成<b>把质子搬进类囊体内腔</b>这件渗透功。书的说法跟呼吸链那一章是同一套：电子传递<b>伴随</b>质子转移，一部分是<b>表观</b>的（一侧消耗、另一侧放出），一部分是<b>真实</b>的（疏水的质体醌真的把质子背过膜）。<b>如果两次推举之间没有落差，就只有 NADPH 没有 ATP——而固定 CO₂ 两样都要。</b>'],
  ['那为什么能量要先存成 <b>ATP 和 NADPH</b>，不直接用光去还原 CO₂？','因为<b>两套过程的时间尺度差了十几个数量级，对不上</b>。书自己把它们分开列了：光的吸收和能量传到反应中心是 <b>10⁻¹⁵ 到 10⁻⁸ 秒，而且跟温度无关</b>；类囊体膜上的生化过程是 <b>10⁻¹⁰ 到 10⁻² 秒</b>；而固定 CO₂ 是一串酶催化反应，<b>依赖温度、慢得多</b>（<span class="pg">p203</span>，已回 400 dpi 核过）。一个飞秒级、不可储存的激发态没法直接驱动一条秒级的酶促通路，所以中间必须有一种<b>可储存、可扩散、化学上稳定</b>的货币——这正是书把 NADPH 和 ATP 叫作「<b>第一批稳定产物</b>」的意思。']
 ],
 stop:{lbl:'停 · 落在热力学（＋一半落在测量的限制）',
  say:'“Below that we are asking how a photon’s energy becomes a separated charge across a membrane in a few picoseconds, and that is photophysics and physical chemistry rather than biochemistry. The textbook itself draws the line in the same place — it says the primary events belong to the <b>physics</b> of photosynthesis, and that biochemistry starts where the redox reactions start.”'}
},

/* ---------------- 同一个答案，不同问法 ---------------- */
recog:[
{q:'What is photosynthesis?',
 mean:'<b>他嘴里多半只有这一句。</b>卷面写的是 “Explain the <b>process</b> of photosynthesis”，口试却从最短的问法起步——<b>剩下的结构要你自己接</b>：是什么 → 总式 → 两半 → 在哪儿 → 然后才是载体的名字。<b>先给定义，不要一上来就 “light reactions and dark reactions”</b>，那是分类不是定义。',
 say:'“It is the biological conversion of <b>radiant energy into chemical energy</b> — light drives the oxidation of a donor and the reduction of carbon dioxide to carbohydrate. In green plants the donor is <b>water</b>, so oxygen is released; the textbook writes the overall equation as twelve waters plus six CO₂ giving a hexose, six oxygens and six waters, at plus 2867 kilojoules per mole, and it writes twelve deliberately, to stress that <b>the oxygen comes from water and not from CO₂</b>. It has two halves: the <b>light reactions</b> in the thylakoid membrane, which give ATP, NADPH and oxygen, and the <b>fixation of CO₂</b> in the stroma, which spends them — the Calvin cycle, beginning at rubisco.”'},

{q:'Explain the process — what happens, in what order?',
 mean:'卷面上的 <b>process</b> 这个词。<b>他要的是顺序，不是名词表。</b>按一条线走，每个载体点名，走到糖为止。',
 say:'“In order: light is absorbed by <b>antenna</b> pigments and the energy is funnelled into a <b>reaction centre</b>, where one chlorophyll gives up an electron. In <b>photosystem two</b>, at P680, the electron goes to <b>pheophytin</b> and on to <b>plastoquinone</b>, and the hole is filled by <b>splitting water</b> at the inner face — that is where the oxygen comes off. Plastoquinone carries the electrons to the <b>cytochrome b6f complex</b>, pumping protons into the thylakoid; <b>plastocyanin</b> takes them to <b>photosystem one</b>, where a second photon re-excites P700; then <b>ferredoxin</b> and <b>ferredoxin–NADP reductase</b> make <b>NADPH</b>. The protons inside flow back out through <b>ATP synthase</b> and make <b>ATP</b>. Then in the stroma, <b>rubisco</b> adds CO₂ to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate to give two <b>3-phosphoglycerates</b>; ATP and NADPH reduce them to <b>triose phosphate</b>; and most of the triose is rearranged back into the acceptor, with the surplus leaving as fructose-6-phosphate.”'},

{q:'What are the light reactions and what do they produce?',
 mean:'<b>把两半拆开问的第一半。</b>要答「产什么」——而且要把 <b>O₂ 是副产物</b>、<b>不固定碳</b> 两件事说进去。',
 say:'“They are the reactions in the <b>thylakoid membrane</b> in which absorbed light drives electrons from water to NADP-plus, through two photosystems in series. Their products are the textbook’s <b>first stable products</b>: <b>NADPH</b>, made by ferredoxin–NADP reductase on the stroma side, and <b>ATP</b>, made by ATP synthase from the proton gradient — plus <b>oxygen</b>, which is a by-product of taking the electrons off water. They fix <b>no carbon at all</b>; carbon enters only in the stroma, at rubisco.”'},

{q:'What are the dark reactions, and when do they happen?',
 mean:'<b>陷阱题的礼貌版本。</b>“when” 就是在钓你说「晚上」。答案要引书自己的反对。',
 say:'“They are the <b>fixation and reduction of CO₂</b> in the stroma — the Calvin cycle. And they happen <b>in the light</b>, like everything else. This textbook explicitly objects to the name: its words are that the term dark reaction is <b>not appropriate, because it suggests the conversion does not occur in the light</b>. It adds that even calling it light-independent is unsatisfactory, because the sequence runs in light or in dark <b>for as long as there is enough NADPH and ATP</b> — and those only come from the light reactions. So dark means <b>not directly requiring photons</b>, not nocturnal.”'},

{q:'What is the Calvin cycle?',
 mean:'<b>这是一道可以单独出的题。</b>定义 → 三阶段 → 第一个产物 → 净产物，四层，别只说 “it fixes CO₂”。',
 say:'“It is the cycle of enzyme reactions in the <b>stroma</b> that fixes CO₂ and reduces it to carbohydrate, using the ATP and NADPH made by the light reactions. Three phases: <b>carboxylation</b>, in which <b>rubisco</b> adds CO₂ to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate and the six-carbon intermediate splits into two <b>3-phosphoglycerates</b> — the first stable product, three carbons, which is why these are C-3 plants; <b>reduction</b> of those to glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, spending ATP and NADPH, by the two glycolytic enzymes running backwards; and <b>regeneration</b> of ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate through the pentose phosphate reactions plus one ATP. The textbook gives the net yield as one <b>fructose-6-phosphate</b>, and it says the reactions were described by <b>Calvin, who had the Nobel Prize in 1961, with Benson and Bassham</b>.”'},

{q:'Where in the cell does each half happen?',
 mean:'<b>区室题。</b>她自己说过区室最容易乱，所以这一行要能不假思索地给出三个名字：<b>类囊体膜 · 内腔 · 基质</b>。',
 say:'“Both halves are in the <b>chloroplast</b>, but in different parts of it. The light reactions are in the <b>thylakoid membrane</b> — the photosystems, the cytochrome b6f complex and the ATP synthase are all integral to it — with water split at the <b>inner</b> surface, into the thylakoid <b>lumen</b>, and NADP-plus reduced at the <b>outer</b> surface. The fixation of CO₂ is in the <b>stroma</b>, the soluble phase outside the thylakoids, where rubisco sits. The textbook’s diagram of a maize chloroplast labels the outer and inner membranes, the intermembrane space, the <b>grana</b> — the stacks of thylakoids — the stroma thylakoids connecting them, the stroma, and the thylakoid lumen.”'},

{q:'Why are plants green?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>听起来像小孩问的问题</b>，答案却是吸收光谱。关键是答「<b>剩下的</b>」而不是「吸收的」。',
 say:'“Because green is the part of the light they <b>do not</b> use. Chlorophyll absorbs in the blue, around 415 to 440 nanometres, and in the red, 640 to 700; the band in between is reflected and transmitted, and that is what reaches your eye. The textbook puts it as a matter of complementary colours — the complementary colour of red is green, and that is why the plant world is green. Its own figure shows the pigments of a higher plant, the chlorophylls and the carotenoids, leaving that middle band open; the pigments that would fill it, the <b>phycobilins</b>, belong to cyanobacteria and red algae.”',
 tail:'<b>他要是追「那绿光完全没用吗」</b>：“Not completely — a leaf is thick, and green light that is not absorbed on the first pass can be absorbed deeper in. But that is general, not from this textbook.”'},

{q:'Why do leaves turn yellow and red in autumn?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>入口像园艺题，答案在色素那一格。</b>要分清「显出来的」和「新造的」两种颜色——这一分，就不是背书的人。',
 say:'“Because the chlorophyll is broken down first and the other pigments become visible. The yellows and oranges are the <b>carotenoids</b> that were there all along as accessory and protective pigments — the textbook names <b>zeaxanthin as the typical yellow pigment of the leaves of higher plants</b> — and they show once the green is gone. The reds are different: they are <b>anthocyanins, newly made</b> in the leaf in autumn, and that part is general knowledge, not in this textbook, which has no anthocyanins. It is a nice illustration that chlorophyll <b>is</b> turned over and degraded, even though it is not consumed by the photochemistry itself.”'},

{q:'Where does the oxygen we breathe come from?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>最朴素的入口，也是这道题最硬的一句。</b>答案要落在「水」上，并给出书为什么那样写式子。',
 say:'“From <b>water</b>, not from carbon dioxide. It is released when photosystem two fills the electron hole in P680 by <b>photolysing water</b> at the inner surface of the thylakoid: two water molecules give four protons, four electrons and one molecule of oxygen. This textbook makes the point by how it writes the equation — twelve waters and six CO₂ rather than six and six — and says in so many words that the stoichiometric factors are given <b>so as to stress that the released oxygen comes from water and not from carbon dioxide</b>. The classical proof is the <b>oxygen-18 labelling experiment</b>: label the water and the heavy oxygen comes out as O₂; label the CO₂ and it does not. That experiment is general knowledge — this book states the conclusion without the experiment.”'},

{q:'Why does a plant need light but my yeast culture does not?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>从她自己的台面进来的问题。</b>答案是自养 vs 异养，而且要落在「电子从哪儿来」上，不是「食物」上。',
 say:'“Because of where the electrons come from. Yeast is <b>heterotrophic</b>: I give it glucose, which is already reduced, and it takes electrons off that. A plant is <b>autotrophic</b> — the textbook’s definition is that it can cover its whole energy requirement from solar energy and build its own substance from CO₂, water and minerals. Its only electron donor is <b>water</b>, and water holds its electrons very tightly: plus 820 millivolts against minus 320 for NADP-plus. Nothing in a cell can pull an electron off water spontaneously, so the energy has to come from outside, and light is what supplies it. Yeast never needs that, because the glucose I feed it has already had the work done on it — by a plant.”',
 tail:'<b>这一条顺势能接回主场</b>：她的酵母培养里那点碳，来源就是某个叶绿体的基质。'},

{q:'Why is a greenhouse warm, and does the warmth help the plant?', odd:1,
 mean:'<b>入口像物理题，其实在问「光反应和暗反应哪个依赖温度」。</b>答对了同时把「暗反应」的名字讲清楚了。',
 say:'“The warmth of a greenhouse is a physical effect, not a photosynthetic one — glass lets visible light in and holds the warmed air and re-radiated heat. But it does help, and the interesting part is <b>which half</b> it helps. The textbook separates them by their physics: the <b>primary events</b>, absorption and energy transfer to the reaction centre, take ten to the minus fifteen up to ten to the minus eight seconds and are <b>independent of temperature</b>; the <b>fixation of CO₂</b> is a chain of enzyme reactions and behaves like any enzyme chain — it speeds up with temperature until the enzymes start to suffer. So warmth accelerates the dark reactions and does nothing for the photochemistry, which is also the cleanest way to see what <b>dark</b> really means in that name.”',
 tail:'<b>他要是追「那为什么太热了反而不好」</b>：“Because rubisco’s oxygenase side wins as it warms — photorespiration rises faster than carboxylation, and the stomata close to save water, which lowers CO₂ inside the leaf. That is the pressure the C-4 plants are built against. General, and the neighbouring question.”'},

{q:'Is photosynthesis just respiration run backwards?',
 mean:'<b>他把两张卡放在一起考的问法。</b>答 “no” 之后必须立刻给<b>四条差别</b>，否则就成了空话。',
 say:'“No — it looks like it on paper and it is not the same path reversed. Four differences. <b>Compartment</b>: chloroplast, thylakoid membrane and stroma, against mitochondrion, inner membrane and matrix. <b>Carrier</b>: photosynthesis reduces <b>NADP-plus</b> and respiration oxidises <b>NADH</b> — this textbook says explicitly that NADPH is <b>not</b> oxidised in the respiratory chain but used for synthetic reactions. <b>Gradient</b>: protons are driven <b>into</b> the thylakoid and out of the matrix, and the book says the thylakoid membrane is oriented the opposite way. <b>Driving force</b>: in respiration the electrons fall from minus 0.32 to plus 0.82 volts on their own; here they are pushed the whole way uphill by two photons. What is genuinely the same is the design — carriers in a membrane, a proton gradient, and an ATP synthase the textbook calls analogous in composition and function.”'}
],

/* ---------------- 追问 ---------------- */
ask:[
{g:'定义类', gn:'逐字稿里没交代完的名词，他会一个一个拎出来问。<b>这一章她没学过，所以这一组要当第一遍学的材料读。</b>', items:[
{r:'高', q:'What is a thylakoid, and what is a granum?',
 en:'“A <b>thylakoid</b> is a closed flattened sac inside the chloroplast — the textbook gives the Greek, <b>thylakos, a sac</b> — and says that, as with the inner mitochondrial membrane, you can distinguish an inner and an outer space. Its membrane carries the whole light apparatus, and its inner space, the <b>lumen</b>, is where protons accumulate. A <b>granum</b> is a <b>stack</b> of thylakoids; the stacks are joined to each other by <b>stroma thylakoids</b>. Everything outside the thylakoids, inside the chloroplast’s two envelope membranes, is the <b>stroma</b>, and that is where CO₂ is fixed.”',
 cn:'<b>「granum」全书正文 grep 0 命中，但 Obr. 9.1（<span class="pg">p203</span>）的标注上有</b>——已回 400 dpi 核过，图上写着 vnější membrána · vnitřní membrána · mezimembránový prostor · <b>granum</b> · stromální thylakoidy · stroma · lumen (vnitřní prostor) thylakoidu。<b>说的时候可以直接说 “the textbook labels the grana in its electron micrograph of a maize chloroplast”</b>——指图比背词有力。<br>⚠ <b>「PS II 在基粒里、PS I 在基质类囊体上」这个分布书里没有，要说就标 general。</b>'},
{r:'高', q:'What exactly is a reaction centre, and what is an antenna?',
 en:'“The <b>reaction centre</b> is a few chlorophyll molecules bound to protein that can actually be <b>photo-oxidised</b> — give up an electron — and the textbook says they can do it because of their <b>microenvironment in the pigment-protein complex</b>, not because they are chemically different. The <b>antenna</b> is all the other chlorophyll, about two hundred molecules per centre; it absorbs light and passes the <b>energy</b> inward, and the book stresses that <b>no charge separation occurs</b> during that transfer. The centre plus its nearest acceptors and donors, its binding proteins and its light-harvesting pigments, is what the book calls a <b>photosystem</b>.”',
 cn:'比值和「微环境」两句<b>已回 400 dpi 核过</b>（<span class="pg">p207</span>）。<b>「传能量不传电子」是这一格的关键区分</b>——天线里没有电荷分离，所以天线色素<b>不被氧化</b>，这也是点 20 第四条陷阱（叶绿素不被消耗）的一半理由。'},
{r:'高', q:'What is P680, and why that number?',
 en:'“<b>P</b> is for pigment and the number is the <b>wavelength in nanometres of its absorption maximum</b>. The textbook explains the convention directly: oxidising P changes its absorption spectrum, so the specific chlorophylls in different reaction centres are named by that wavelength — <b>P870 in bacteria, P680 and P700 in oxygenic photosynthesis</b>. P680 is the reaction-centre pigment of photosystem two, P700 of photosystem one.”',
 cn:'下标<b>已回 400 dpi 从 Obr. 9.5 图注和正文核过</b>（OCR 把它们全吃了，grep 「P700」0 命中——<b>这是 OCR 的错，不是书的错，不进勘误</b>）。<b>「名字是怎么来的」这种小题他很喜欢问</b>，因为答得出就说明不是硬背的。<span class="pg">p207</span>'},
{r:'中', q:'What is plastoquinone, and what is plastocyanin? They sound alike.',
 en:'“Different molecules doing different jobs. <b>Plastoquinone</b> is a small <b>lipid-soluble quinone</b>, not a protein; it moves <b>inside</b> the membrane carrying <b>two electrons together with two protons</b>, which is what makes it the real proton carrier across the thylakoid membrane. The textbook says it is the plant counterpart of <b>coenzyme Q</b> in the respiratory chain. <b>Plastocyanin</b> is a small <b>copper protein</b> — the book gives it a mass of ten to twenty kilodaltons, an absorption maximum near <b>597 nanometres</b>, a standard potential at pH 7 of <b>370 millivolts</b>, and says it is <b>blue</b> — which slides along the inner face carrying <b>one</b> electron at a time, the job cytochrome c does in the mitochondrion.”',
 cn:'四个参数<b>已回 400 dpi 核过</b>（<span class="pg">p206</span>）；辅酶 Q 的对应在 <span class="pg">p045</span>。<b>「一个带两个电子、一个带一个电子」跟卡 25 是同一个结构</b>——两电子载体和单电子载体之间必须有个复合物把它们对上，那里就是 <b>cyt b₆f</b>（线粒体里是复合物 III）。'},
{r:'中', q:'What is ferredoxin?',
 en:'“A small <b>iron–sulfur protein</b> — the textbook defines the class on the coenzyme pages: the iron is bound to the protein by the <b>-SH groups of cysteine and by free sulfide ions</b>, not by a porphyrin, so it is <b>non-heme</b> iron, and it carries electrons by the iron changing between Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺. It names ferredoxin as its example and says these proteins work as electron carriers <b>in respiration and in photosynthesis</b>. In the chloroplast it sits on the <b>stroma</b> side, takes the electron from photosystem one, and hands it to ferredoxin–NADP reductase.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p046</span>。<b>顺手多给一条</b>：书 §9.4（<span class="pg">p216</span>）说还原硝酸盐也要光合来的<b>还原型铁氧还蛋白</b>——所以 Fd 不只是通往 NADPH 的一站，它是叶绿体的<b>还原力分配点</b>。这一条能把「光合除了糖还干什么」答得漂亮。'},
{r:'中', q:'What does “photolysis” mean?',
 en:'“Splitting by light — here, <b>the light-driven splitting of water</b>. The textbook lists it as part of the biochemistry of photosynthesis: the whole complex of processes connected with the transfer of electrons and protons, <b>including the photolysis of water</b>. Strictly it is not the photon that breaks the bond: the photon oxidises P680, P680-plus is a strong enough oxidant to take electrons from water, and the water then falls apart into protons and oxygen. Two waters give four protons, four electrons and one O₂, at the inner surface of the thylakoid.”',
 cn:'<b>「不是光子直接劈开水」这一层值钱</b>，因为 photolysis 这个词本身在误导。书没有明说这一层，<b>但它是书自己那套顺序的必然推论</b>（P 先被氧化，空位再由次级供体填上，<span class="pg">p208</span>）。说的时候可以带一句 “that follows from the book’s own order of events”。'}
]},

{g:'为什么类', gn:'卡上给了结论没给理由的地方 —— 他插刀就插在这里。', items:[
{r:'高', q:'Why are two photosystems needed?',
 en:'“Because the electron has to go a long way uphill and one photon will not pay for it. The textbook gives both ends: the oxygen–water couple at <b>plus 820 millivolts</b>, NADP-plus over NADPH at <b>minus 320</b>. That is 1.14 volts <b>against</b> the spontaneous direction, and it is why the scheme is drawn as a Z — two photon-driven jumps in series with a downhill run between them. The downhill run is not wasted: it is where the protons are moved and the ATP comes from.”',
 cn:'两个电位<b>已回 400 dpi 核过</b>，而且跟书自己 Tab. 6.2 里氧的 <b>+0,82 V</b> 一致（<span class="pg">p135</span>）——<b>这是书内部自洽</b>，不是我们算的。1,14 V 是相减得到的，<b>说的时候带一句「按你这本书的两个数算下来」</b>。<b>「中间那段下坡是挣 ATP 的」这半句是通用推论</b>，书只写了质子转移跟电子传递相伴。'},
{r:'高', q:'Why does the plant bother making NADPH and ATP instead of using the light directly on CO₂?',
 en:'“Because the two halves work on time scales that cannot be coupled directly. The textbook separates them: absorption and energy transfer to the reaction centre take <b>ten to the minus fifteen to ten to the minus eight seconds and are independent of temperature</b>; the membrane biochemistry takes <b>ten to the minus ten to ten to the minus two</b>; and fixing CO₂ is a chain of enzyme reactions, slower still and temperature-dependent. An excited state that lasts femtoseconds cannot drive an enzyme chain, so the energy has to be parked in something <b>stable, diffusible and chemical</b> — and that is exactly how the book describes NADPH and ATP: the <b>first stable products</b>, in which the electromagnetic energy of the photons is stored as chemical energy.”',
 cn:'时间尺度<b>已回 400 dpi 核过</b>（<span class="pg">p203</span>）。<b>这是全卡最好的一条 why</b>，因为它的三层全在书里，不用借外部权威；而且它一句话同时解释了「为什么分两半」和「为什么叫暗反应」。'},
{r:'高', q:'Why is the proton gradient the other way round from the mitochondrion?',
 en:'“Because the membrane itself is oriented the other way round, and the textbook says so in those words: <b>the orientation of thylakoid membranes is reversed</b>. In the mitochondrion protons are pumped <b>out</b> of the matrix and the synthase head faces the matrix; in the chloroplast protons are driven <b>into</b> the thylakoid lumen and the synthase knobs protrude on the <b>outer</b> surface, letting protons through from inside out into the stroma. The book even offers the reason: the explanation <b>lies in the evolution of the membranes and their different pinching off and self-closing</b>. So the chemistry is the same and the topology is inverted.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p208 p209</span>。<b>这一条是这道题最容易说反的地方，也是他很爱问的地方</b>——因为说反了说明你在背图而不是懂图。<b>一个记法</b>：两台机器的 ATP 都造在<b>细胞质那一侧的可溶相里</b>（线粒体基质、叶绿体基质），因为 ATP 要用在那里；所以质子总是<b>朝着「另一边」</b>被搬走。<b>后半句是通用记法，不是书说的。</b>'},
{r:'中', q:'Why does the cell keep NADPH and NADH apart at all?',
 en:'“Because it uses them for opposite jobs and wants to set their ratios independently. This textbook states both halves: it says the NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway <b>is not oxidised in the respiratory chain but is used for synthetic reactions</b>, and, comparing fatty-acid synthesis with degradation, that <b>the hydrogen donor for synthesis is NADPH whereas oxidation produces NADH</b>. The two differ by a single phosphate on the ribose, and that one group is enough for enzymes to tell them apart — so the cell can hold NADH mostly oxidised, to keep catabolism running, and NADPH mostly reduced, to keep biosynthesis running.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p219 p220</span>。<b>「一个磷酸就把两条路分开了」这句话在卡 25 里已经有了</b>，两边说的是同一件事，背一次用两次。<b>「独立设定比例」这半句是通用推论。</b>'},
{r:'中', q:'Why does the antenna need to be so large?',
 en:'“Because photons arrive at any one chlorophyll rarely, while the reaction centre is expensive and should never be idle. About two hundred antenna molecules feed one centre, so the centre sees a photon’s worth of energy often enough to keep working. The textbook adds the other half of the argument from the centre’s side: the <b>quantum yield of the charge separation is high</b>, meaning this route of de-excitation is <b>strongly preferred over fluorescence or conversion into heat</b> — so almost all the energy the antenna delivers is actually used.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p207</span>。<b>「昂贵的中心 ＋ 便宜的天线」这个说法是通用的</b>，书只给了比值和量子产率高这两件事实。<b>反过来还能接一句</b>：天线太大也危险——光太强时多余的激发能会生成活性氧，这正是类胡萝卜素要当抗氧化剂的原因（<span class="pg">p187</span>）。'}
]},

{g:'怎么发现的', gn:'<b>Calvin 那一条书自己给了，氧来自水那一条书只给结论。</b>两条一起说，一条有出处一条标 general，反而显得你分得清。', items:[
{r:'中', q:'How do we know the oxygen comes from water and not from CO₂?',
 en:'“By <b>labelling the oxygen</b>. Grow the cells with water enriched in <b>oxygen-18</b> and ordinary CO₂, and the O₂ that comes off is heavy; do it the other way round, heavy CO₂ and ordinary water, and the O₂ is not. So the oxygen atoms released trace back to water. That experiment is <b>general knowledge — this textbook states the conclusion but not the experiment</b>; what it does is write the equation with twelve waters instead of six, saying in so many words that the stoichiometric factors are there to stress that the released oxygen comes from water and not from carbon dioxide.”',
 cn:'全书 grep：<b>izotop 0 · 18O 0 · Ruben 0 · Kamen 0 · Hill 0</b>（p229 那个是 McGraw-Hill）。<b>说的时候明确标 general。</b><br><b>第二条证据也值得会</b>（同样 general）：<b>Hill 反应</b>——离体叶绿体在没有 CO₂ 的情况下，只要给一个人工电子受体就照样放氧。<b>没有 CO₂ 也能放氧，就说明氧不可能来自 CO₂。</b>英文：“And the Hill reaction: isolated chloroplasts evolve oxygen with an artificial electron acceptor and no CO₂ present at all — oxygen release does not need CO₂, so it cannot come from it.”'},
{r:'中', q:'Who worked out the Calvin cycle, and how?',
 en:'“The textbook names them: the reactions of CO₂ fixation were described by <b>M. Calvin, who received the Nobel Prize in 1961, with A. A. Benson and J. A. Bassham</b>, and it says the pathway is often called by their names or simply the Calvin cycle. The method is general rather than from this book: they fed illuminated algae <b>radioactive carbon-14 dioxide</b> for a few seconds, killed them, separated the soluble compounds by <b>two-dimensional paper chromatography</b> and found which spots were radioactive. The shorter the exposure, the fewer the labelled compounds — and at the shortest times essentially all the label was in <b>3-phosphoglycerate</b>, which is how it was identified as the first stable product.”',
 cn:'人名和 1961 年<b>书上有</b>（<span class="pg">p209</span>；Benson 在 OCR 里被断行成 “Ben-sonem” 所以 grep 不到，<b>原文有</b>）。<b>¹⁴C、二维纸层析、那个 lollipop 装置全部 general</b>，说的时候标出来。<br><b>这条追问的价值不在历史</b>：「<b>缩短标记时间，看谁先亮</b>」这个思路本身就是做通路的通用方法，说得出这一层比记住人名值钱得多。'},
{r:'中', q:'Where does the chemiosmotic idea come from?',
 en:'“From <b>Peter Mitchell</b>, in 1961 — the same principle for the mitochondrion and the chloroplast: the thing connecting electron transport to ATP synthesis is not a chemical intermediate but a <b>proton gradient across a membrane</b>. He had the Nobel Prize in 1978. That is general knowledge; this textbook gives the principle without the name, and it applies it to both membranes — it says the thylakoid ATP synthase is analogous in composition and function to the mitochondrial one, and that the only difference is the orientation.”',
 cn:'全书 grep：<b>Mitchell 0 · chemiosmot 0</b>，跟卡 <b>25</b> 完全一样（那张卡上已经有这一条）。<b>两张卡共用同一个答案，背一次用两次。</b><br><b>叶绿体这一侧还有一个更漂亮的实验（general）</b>：把离体类囊体在酸性缓冲液里泡到内腔变酸，再突然移到碱性缓冲液里，<b>在完全黑暗中它们就会合成 ATP</b>——只靠一个人为造出来的 pH 差。<b>这是化学渗透最干净的证明，因为它把光整个拿掉了。</b>'}
]},

{g:'陷阱', gn:'这几条几乎每次都会被问，而且都是一句话说错就掉分的那种。', items:[
{r:'高', q:'So the oxygen comes from the carbon dioxide?',
 en:'“No — it comes from <b>water</b>. Photosystem two fills the electron hole in P680 by oxidising water at the inner surface of the thylakoid, and the oxygen is what is left when the electrons and protons have been taken off it. The carbon dioxide goes the other way entirely: it is <b>reduced</b>, in the stroma, and both of its oxygens end up in the sugar and in water. This textbook writes the overall equation with twelve waters on the left rather than six <b>precisely to make this point</b>.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p203</span>。<b>这是这道题被问得最多的一句。</b>说错了不只是一个事实错——它说明你把光反应和暗反应接反了。<b>一个自检</b>：CO₂ 是<b>受体</b>（书的通式里的 A），水是<b>供体</b>（H₂D）；供体被氧化，所以氧从供体来。'},
{r:'高', q:'Do the dark reactions happen at night?',
 en:'“No, and the textbook says the name is a bad one. Its words: the term <b>dark phase is not appropriate, because it suggests that this conversion does not occur in the light</b>. Nor is light-independent much better, because the sequence runs in light or in darkness <b>only for as long as there is enough NADPH and ATP</b> — and those come from the light reactions, so in practice the cycle runs in daylight, right alongside them. Dark means <b>not directly requiring photons</b>.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p209</span>。<b>能引用书自己反对这个名字，比自己反对有力得多。</b>顺带能挡住「那植物晚上做什么」——<b>呼吸</b>，它有线粒体，跟你一样。'},
{r:'高', q:'So the light reactions make the sugar?',
 en:'“No — the light reactions make <b>ATP, NADPH and oxygen</b>, and not one carbon atom. The textbook calls NADPH and ATP the <b>first stable products</b> of the thylakoid processes and then says those two compounds are <b>used above all in the fixation and reduction of CO₂</b>, which is a separate set of enzyme reactions in the stroma. Carbon enters the pathway at exactly one place: <b>rubisco</b>, adding CO₂ to ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p209</span>。<b>这一条跟卡 25 那条「链不造 ATP，合酶才造」是同一种错法</b>：把一台机器的两半混成一件事。<b>两张卡的这一条要一起记。</b>'},
{r:'中', q:'Is chlorophyll used up?',
 en:'“No — it is a <b>catalyst, not a substrate</b>. The reaction-centre chlorophyll gives up an electron and gets one back within a very short time, from water by way of the oxygen-evolving complex in photosystem two, or from plastocyanin in photosystem one; the antenna chlorophyll is <b>never oxidised at all</b>, it only passes energy on. So the molecule goes round a cycle and is not consumed by the photochemistry. It <b>is</b> degraded and replaced over time, which is why leaves go yellow in autumn when the chlorophyll is broken down and the carotenoids show — but that is turnover, not consumption by the reaction.”',
 cn:'书没有直接写「叶绿素不被消耗」，<b>但它写全了理由</b>：天线色素 <i>se neoxidují</i>（不被氧化，<span class="pg">p207</span>），反应中心的空位由次级供体填上（<span class="pg">p208</span>）。<b>说的时候用书的两句话推，比下一个断言稳。</b>秋天那半句是 general。'},
{r:'中', q:'Is the chloroplast ATP synthase a different enzyme from the mitochondrial one?',
 en:'“No — the textbook says its <b>composition and function are again analogous</b> to the ATP synthase of the inner mitochondrial membrane, and its figure names the parts <b>CF-zero and CF-one</b> against F-zero and F-one, with the C for chloroplast. The mechanism is the same: protons flow down their gradient through the membrane part, and the head releases the ATP. The one thing that differs is the <b>orientation</b> — the knobs face the stroma and the protons come out of the thylakoid, whereas in the mitochondrion the knobs face the matrix and the protons go in.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p208 p209</span>。<b>「同一台机器装反了」是最省事的说法</b>，而且它让卡 25 的合酶那两格（结构、把 ATP 松开的机制）在这道题上原样可用——<b>不用再背一遍</b>。'}
]},

{g:'相邻考点', gn:'从这道题走出去，他多半走这四个方向。每个一句话就能接住，不要展开。', items:[
{r:'高', q:'What are C-3, C-4 and CAM plants?',
 en:'“<b>C-3</b> plants are the ones fixing CO₂ by the Calvin cycle, and the textbook says they are named after the <b>first stable product, the three-carbon 3-phosphoglycerate</b>. <b>C-4</b> plants, discovered by <b>Hatch and Slack in 1970</b>, first fix CO₂ onto <b>phosphoenolpyruvate</b> with <b>PEP carboxylase</b>, giving the four-carbon <b>oxaloacetate</b>, which becomes malate or aspartate; that is carried to the bundle-sheath cells, decarboxylated, and the CO₂ released there is fixed again by rubisco in an ordinary Calvin cycle. The point of the detour is that <b>PEP carboxylase works at lower CO₂ concentrations — a lower Km — than rubisco needs</b>, so the leaf can keep its stomata almost closed and still feed rubisco; the book’s phrase is that the mechanism <b>concentrates CO₂</b>. That is the next question on the list, so I will leave it there unless you want it.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p212 p213 p214</span> —— <b>这是题库 #28，是另一张卡，这里只碰一下。</b>CAM 那一半（时间上分开而不是空间上分开）书在 <span class="pg">p215</span> 附近，<b>这张卡不承诺细节</b>。<b>最后那半句「除非你想听」是故意留的口子</b>：它落在一道你知道边界的题上，不是深坑。'},
{r:'高', q:'How does this compare with the respiratory chain?',
 en:'“The textbook draws the comparison itself. It says the structure of the thylakoid membrane is <b>closest, structurally and functionally, to the inner mitochondrial membrane, the seat of the respiratory chain</b>, and that the carriers are analogous: <b>quinones, cytochromes, iron–sulfur proteins, flavoproteins</b>, and a nicotinamide coenzyme. Plants have two extras: <b>plastocyanin</b>, a copper protein, and the oxygen-evolving complex. Three real differences: <b>light pushes the electrons uphill</b> instead of letting them fall; the product is <b>NADPH</b>, for biosynthesis, rather than NADH for oxidation; and the protons go <b>into</b> the closed compartment instead of out of it.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p206 p208</span>。<b>书自己把两者并排写了，所以这是有出处的类比，不是你的发挥</b>——卡 <b>25</b> 的相邻考点那一格也有这条，两边对得上。<b>接住之后就走进卡 25</b>，那张卡她已经背过。'},
{r:'中', q:'What happens to the fructose-6-phosphate afterwards?',
 en:'“The textbook takes it to <b>glucose-6-phosphate</b> and then three ways: on to <b>glucose</b>, on to <b>cellulose</b>, or into the <b>general metabolic pathways — it names glycolysis</b>. And it warns against thinking hexose is the point: it says the larger part of the fixed carbon is delivered into the <b>central metabolic pathways, naming the citric acid cycle</b>, and serves as a chemical source of energy and of carbon for the biosynthesis of other sugars, amino acids, proteins, fatty acids, lipids, nucleotides, nucleic acids, pigments and secondary plant metabolites.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p209 p211 p212</span>。<b>这一条把光合接回她背过的糖那一块</b>（卡 <b>06</b> 的葡萄糖、卡 <b>gly</b> 的糖酵解）：<b>Calvin 循环吐出来的那个 F6P，就是糖酵解第二步的产物</b>——同一个分子，两条路在这里对接。'},
{r:'中', q:'Does photosynthesis do anything besides making sugar?',
 en:'“Yes, and the textbook is explicit about it. Its opening list of the metabolisms specific to autotrophs has five items, of which photosynthesis proper is only the first: <b>assimilation of atmospheric nitrogen; reduction of nitrates, nitrites and sulfates and the biosynthesis of cysteine; biosynthesis of aromatic amino acids; and the formation of secondary plant metabolites</b>. And it shows the link: reducing nitrite in a leaf needs a strong reductant of low redox potential, and it says the source of the reduced <b>ferredoxin</b> for that is <b>photosynthesis</b>. So the light reactions supply reducing power to the whole plant, not just to the Calvin cycle.”',
 cn:'<span class="pg">p202 p215 p216</span>（§9.4）。<b>这一条的价值是把这道题从「一条通路」升成「自养生物的能量入口」</b>——而且全部有出处。<b>但不要往氮代谢里走</b>，它是另一节，说完这句就停。'}
]},

{g:'桥回主场', gn:'段 4 那条桥，被追问时的第二层。<b>接住了就落在定向进化和酵母展示上——她的日常工作。</b>', items:[
{r:'中', q:'You engineer proteins — would you try to improve rubisco?',
 en:'“It is the classic example of a target that looks obvious and has resisted for decades, so I would be careful about promising anything. The problem is not that nobody has tried; it is that the property you want and the property you can select for are coupled. Across natural rubiscos, the variants that <b>discriminate better between CO₂ and O₂ tend to be the slower ones</b> — so a screen that selects for specificity gives back a slow enzyme, and one that selects for rate gives back a wasteful one. That trade-off is general knowledge, not from this textbook; what the book gives is the two activities in one enzyme and the fact that the oxygenase reaction <b>competes for the same substrate</b>. If I did take it on, the design question would be the one I actually face: <b>what does my selection report on</b>, and can I put both axes into it at once.”',
 cn:'<b>这是这张卡上最诚实的一座桥</b>：不是「我也做光合」，而是「这是我认得的那类问题」。<b>两个 general 必须标出来</b>：权衡本身，以及「地球上最丰富的蛋白」这个最高级（书写的是「之一」）。<br><b>还有一个真实的技术障碍可以加</b>（也是 general）：rubisco 的大亚基是<b>叶绿体基因组</b>编码的，而且折叠要专门的伴侣蛋白，所以它<b>很难在大肠杆菌或酵母里做常规的文库筛选</b>——这一句能解释「为什么试了这么多年还没成」，而且正好落在她的方法学上。'},
{r:'中', q:'Two hundred antenna molecules per reaction centre — is that anything like what you do?',
 en:'“It is the same accounting problem, yes. The antenna exists because the expensive part — the reaction centre — must never be idle, so the cell surrounds it with cheap collectors. In <b>yeast display</b> I have the same ratio problem from the other side: each cell carries many copies of one variant on its surface, and the number of copies sets how strong a signal I get, while the thing I actually want to measure is a property of <b>one</b> molecule. If copy number varies between cells I end up selecting for expression rather than for binding — so I normalise against it. Different system, same lesson: <b>when you amplify a signal, make sure you know which quantity you have amplified</b>.”',
 cn:'<b>诚实地短。</b>不点具体病原体、不展开课题（见 `DRILL.md` 的三条机制）。<b>这条桥的落点是卡 02（Kd）和横向卡 fitness landscape</b>——「我测的到底是哪一个量」正是那两张卡的核心。'}
]}
]
});
